 	Prohibits retaliation for filing a complaint with OCR or for advocating for a right protected by the Act.', '', 'Civil Rights', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:08.228964', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Age Discrimination in Employment Act of 1967 (ADEA), codified at 29 U.S.C. §§ 621-634, prohibits employment discrimination against individuals who are 40 years of age or older. For higher education institutions, this federal statute creates comprehensive obligations to ensure age-neutral employment practices across all personnel decisions.

The ADEA''s core provisions, outlined in 29 U.S.C. § 623, make it unlawful for universities to fail or refuse to hire, discharge, or otherwise discriminate against any individual aged 40 or older regarding compensation, terms, conditions, or privileges of employment because of age. This extends to faculty hiring, tenure decisions, administrative appointments, staff promotions, and retirement policies. The statute applies to educational institutions with 20 or more employees engaged in interstate commerce.

Key regulatory provisions under 29 CFR Part 1625 establish specific guidelines for higher education. Section 1625.7 addresses bona fide occupational qualifications, which are extremely narrow exceptions rarely applicable in academic settings. Universities cannot establish mandatory retirement ages for most positions, following amendments in the Older Workers Benefit Protection Act of 1990.

The Equal Employment Opportunity Commission (EEOC) enforces the ADEA pursuant to 29 U.S.C. § 626. Universities face significant penalties including back pay, front pay, liquidated damages equal to unpaid wages, and attorney fees under 29 U.S.C. § 626(b). The statute of limitations requires filing charges within 180 days of the discriminatory act, extended to 300 days in states with age discrimination laws.

For higher education institutions, compliance challenges often arise in faculty searches, where age-neutral language in job postings is mandatory. Terms like "recent graduates," "digital natives," or "energetic" may constitute evidence of age bias. Tenure and promotion decisions must focus on merit-based criteria documented through objective performance evaluations.

The ADEA intersects with other employment laws in academic settings. While 29 U.S.C. § 631(c) permits mandatory retirement for bona fide executives at age 65, this exemption rarely applies to faculty or standard administrative positions in universities. Recent EEOC enforcement priorities emphasize systemic age discrimination in hiring, making proactive compliance essential for institutional risk management.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:08.228964', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-237', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

Under 20 U.S.C. § 1092(a)(1)(L) and implementing regulations at 34 CFR § 668.41, institutions must annually disclose diversity statistics including enrollment data by race, ethnicity, gender, and other demographic characteristics. These statistics must be prominently displayed and made available to all prospective students during recruitment and enrollment processes.

Retention rate disclosures, mandated by 20 U.S.C. § 1092(a)(1)(R) and detailed in 34 CFR § 668.45, require institutions to report student persistence data including first-time, full-time undergraduate retention rates and completion percentages. Institutions must calculate these rates using standardized federal methodologies and present data in accessible formats.

Employment placement data requirements under 20 U.S.C. § 1092(d) and 34 CFR § 668.42 mandate disclosure of job placement rates, employment statistics, and salary information for graduates by program. For career-focused programs, institutions must provide specific employment outcomes within designated timeframes post-graduation.

Fire safety reporting, governed by 20 U.S.C. § 1092(i) and 34 CFR § 668.49, requires annual security reports including fire statistics, safety systems information, and emergency procedures for campus housing facilities. These reports must be distributed to campus community members and made publicly available.

Transfer of credit policies, required under 20 U.S.C. § 1092(a)(1)(T) and 34 CFR § 668.43, must clearly articulate institutional policies regarding acceptance of transfer credits, including specific criteria, limitations, and procedures. Institutions must disclose any transfer agreements with other institutions.

The Department of Education enforces compliance through program reviews, audits, and potential penalties including fines up to $55,000 per violation under 34 CFR § 668.83. Recent amendments through the FAFSA Simplification Act have enhanced disclosure requirements effective for the 2024-2025 academic year, particularly regarding outcome data transparency and standardized reporting formats.

Institutions must ensure disclosures are prominently displayed on websites, included in promotional materials, and readily accessible to students. Non-compliance may result in loss of Title IV eligibility, civil monetary penalties, and required corrective actions under federal oversight.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:08.270423', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-214', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

Under 20 U.S.C. § 1015(b)(1), institutions must disclose, for each course listed in the institution''s course schedule: (A) the International Standard Book Number (ISBN) and retail price information of required and recommended college textbooks and supplemental materials; (B) information on rental options, used book availability, and digital alternatives; and (C) the copyright dates of all required materials. This information must be made available to students during course registration periods and no later than the time of course enrollment.

The scope of coverage extends to all degree-granting institutions participating in Title IV federal student aid programs under 20 U.S.C. § 1001. Per 34 C.F.R. § 668.41(a), institutions must publish textbook information on their websites or course registration systems in a format that allows students to search and compare costs across multiple sections and instructors. The regulation specifically requires disclosure of both new and used prices when available, rental options, and any digital access codes or online components that may expire.

Institutions must also comply with 20 U.S.C. § 1015(b)(2), which requires faculty to provide textbook adoption information to campus bookstores and administrators as early as practicable, but no later than 60 days before the start of each academic term when feasible. This provision aims to maximize used book availability and reduce student costs.

Enforcement mechanisms include monitoring by the Department of Education during program reviews and compliance audits. Violations may result in corrective action requirements, fine assessments under 34 C.F.R. § 668.83, or potential suspension from Title IV programs for severe or repeated non-compliance. Recent guidance from the Department emphasizes the importance of accurate pricing information and timely updates when material requirements change.

The regulation was strengthened by the Higher Education Opportunity Act of 2008, which added specific timing requirements and expanded disclosure obligations. Institutions must maintain documentation demonstrating compliance with disclosure timelines and accuracy of pricing information for federal audit purposes.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:08.299236', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-251', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

 	Sections 152 and 153 require the following disclosures to enrolled students:
c) transfer of credit policies; . . .





f) diversity statistics;
g) retention rates;
h) employment placement data;
i) fire safety report; . . .
k) along with the course schedule, the International Standard Book Number (ISBN) of every required and recommended textbook and supplemental material and retail price info . . .

Disclosures c) - k) must be available to prospective students as well.', '', 'Academic Programs', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:08.461399', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Higher Education Opportunity Act of 2008, Public Law No. 110-315, enacted significant amendments to the Higher Education Act of 1965, with Sections 152 and 153 establishing comprehensive disclosure requirements that fundamentally transformed transparency obligations for institutions of higher education. These provisions, codified primarily in 20 U.S.C. § 1092, mandate extensive information sharing with both enrolled and prospective students across multiple domains of institutional operations, creating a regulatory framework designed to enhance consumer protection and informed decision-making in higher education.

Section 152 of the Higher Education Opportunity Act, which amended Section 485 of the Higher Education Act of 1965, establishes the foundational disclosure requirements that institutions must satisfy to maintain eligibility for federal student aid programs. The statute requires institutions participating in Title IV federal student aid programs to make available to enrolled students and prospective students detailed information regarding institutional policies, procedures, and outcomes. These disclosure obligations represent a significant expansion of previous transparency requirements and reflect Congress''s intent to provide students and families with comprehensive information necessary for making informed educational investment decisions.

The transfer of credit policies disclosure requirement, codified in 20 U.S.C. § 1092(a)(1)(T), mandates that institutions provide clear, detailed information regarding their policies for accepting transfer credits from other institutions. This disclosure must include specific criteria used to evaluate transfer credits, any limitations on the number or types of credits that may be transferred, and the process by which transfer credit decisions are made. Institutions must articulate their policies regarding acceptance of credits from regionally accredited institutions, nationally accredited institutions, and foreign institutions. The disclosure must also address any articulation agreements with other institutions and specify how students may appeal transfer credit decisions. This requirement addresses longstanding concerns about transparency in credit transfer processes and helps students understand potential impacts on degree completion timelines and costs.

Diversity statistics disclosure requirements, established under 20 U.S.C. § 1092(a)(1)(V), require institutions to provide comprehensive demographic information about their student body and, where applicable, faculty and staff. These statistics must be disaggregated by race, ethnicity, gender, and other relevant demographic categories as specified by the Department of Education. Institutions must present this information in a format that allows prospective and enrolled students to understand the composition of the campus community. The diversity statistics must be updated annually and made readily accessible through institutional websites and other appropriate channels. This requirement supports informed decision-making by students who may consider campus diversity as a factor in their educational choices.

Retention rates disclosure, codified in 20 U.S.C. § 1092(a)(1)(B), requires institutions to provide detailed information about student persistence and completion. Institutions must disclose retention rates for full-time and part-time students, disaggregated by various demographic categories including race, ethnicity, gender, and Pell Grant recipient status. The retention rate calculations must follow specific methodologies established by the Department of Education and typically measure the percentage of first-time, degree-seeking students who return for their second year of study. Institutions must also provide completion or graduation rates, calculated according to federal specifications, which track cohorts of students through to degree completion within specified timeframes. These disclosures must be presented in a clear, understandable format that allows students to assess institutional effectiveness in supporting student success.

Employment placement data requirements, established under 20 U.S.C. § 1092(d), mandate that institutions disclose information about graduate employment outcomes, particularly for programs that prepare students for specific occupations. Institutions must provide data on employment rates, types of employment obtained by graduates, and salary information where available and appropriate. For programs leading to professional licensure or certification, institutions must disclose pass rates on relevant examinations and any other outcome measures that indicate program effectiveness in preparing students for their intended careers. This information must be presented in a manner that allows prospective students to evaluate the potential return on their educational investment and make informed decisions about program selection.

Fire safety report requirements, codified in 20 U.S.C. § 1092(i), mandate comprehensive annual reporting on campus fire safety policies, procedures, and incidents. Institutions must maintain detailed records of all fires occurring in on-campus student housing facilities and compile annual fire safety reports that include statistics on fire incidents, descriptions of fire safety systems in residential facilities, policies regarding portable electrical appliances and smoking, procedures for student housing evacuation, and fire safety education programs. The fire safety report must be made available to current and prospective students and employees and must be submitted to the Department of Education according to specified timelines. Institutions must also maintain a fire log that records all fire incidents and is available for public inspection.

Textbook information disclosure requirements, established under 20 U.S.C. § 1092(a)(1)(T), mandate that institutions provide detailed information about required and recommended course materials in conjunction with course schedule information. Institutions must disclose the International Standard Book Number (ISBN) and retail price information for every required and recommended textbook and supplemental material for each course section. This information must be made available to students at the time of course registration or as early as practicable, allowing students to comparison shop for textbooks and make informed decisions about course material purchases. The disclosure must clearly distinguish between required and recommended materials and must include information about digital or alternative format availability where applicable.

Section 153 of the Higher Education Opportunity Act extends these disclosure requirements to prospective students, ensuring that individuals considering enrollment have access to the same comprehensive information available to enrolled students. This provision recognizes that prospective students need detailed institutional information to make informed enrollment decisions and establishes clear obligations for institutions to provide transparent, accessible information throughout the recruitment and admissions process.

Compliance obligations under these provisions require institutions to establish comprehensive systems for collecting, maintaining, and disseminating required information. Institutions must designate appropriate personnel responsible for ensuring compliance with disclosure requirements and must implement procedures for regular review and updating of disclosed information. The accuracy and completeness of disclosed information is critical, as institutions may face significant penalties for providing false or misleading information to students and the Department of Education.

Reporting and disclosure deadlines vary depending on the specific requirement, but institutions must generally ensure that information is current, accurate, and readily accessible to students and prospective students. Annual reports, such as fire safety reports, must be completed and distributed by October 1 of each year, covering the previous calendar year''s data. Textbook information must be available at the time students register for courses or as early as practicable before the beginning of each academic term. Retention and graduation rate information must be updated annually and made available through institutional websites and other appropriate channels.

The Department of Education has established specific formatting and presentation requirements for many of these disclosures to ensure consistency and comparability across institutions. Institutions must follow prescribed methodologies for calculating rates and statistics and must present information in standardized formats that facilitate student understanding and comparison shopping among institutions.

Penalties for non-compliance with these disclosure requirements can be severe and may include fines, limitations on federal student aid eligibility, or complete loss of Title IV program participation. The Department of Education conducts regular compliance reviews and may impose sanctions on institutions that fail to meet disclosure obligations. Additionally, institutions may face legal liability from students or other parties who suffer harm as a result of inadequate or inaccurate disclosures.

Implementation guidance from the Department of Education emphasizes the importance of making disclosures readily accessible and understandable to students. Institutions are encouraged to use multiple communication channels, including websites, printed materials, and direct communication with students, to ensure that required information reaches its intended audience. The Department has also provided technical assistance and resources to help institutions develop compliant disclosure systems and procedures.

Institutions must also ensure that their disclosure practices comply with other applicable federal and state laws, including privacy regulations that may limit the disclosure of certain types of information. The Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act (FERPA) and other privacy laws may impose constraints on how institutions collect and disclose student information, requiring careful coordination between disclosure obligations and privacy protections.

The regulatory framework established by Sections 152 and 153 reflects a broader federal policy emphasis on transparency and accountability in higher education. These requirements are designed to empower students and families with information necessary for making informed educational decisions while also creating incentives for institutional improvement in areas such as student retention, graduation rates, and employment outcomes.

Institutions should establish comprehensive compliance programs that address all aspects of these disclosure requirements, including regular training for relevant personnel, systematic procedures for data collection and verification, and ongoing monitoring of disclosure practices. Legal counsel should be consulted to ensure that institutional policies and procedures fully comply with federal requirements and to address any questions about the interpretation or application of specific provisions.

The continuing evolution of higher education regulation suggests that disclosure requirements may be subject to future modifications or expansions. Institutions should monitor regulatory developments and be prepared to adapt their compliance programs as necessary to address new or modified requirements. The fundamental principle underlying these regulations—that students deserve access to comprehensive, accurate information about their educational options—is likely to remain a central feature of federal higher education policy, making robust compliance systems an essential component of institutional operations.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:08.461399', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-138', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

