1. Submit annual institutional report cards with achievement data for teacher shortage area placement goals
2. Report state teacher licensing examination pass rates, average scores, and comparative performance data
3. Document clinical experience hours, admissions criteria, and GPA requirements for all programs
4. Provide current accreditation status and any state low-performing program designations
5. Include technology integration training activities and special education preparation components', 'Academic Programs', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:08.52128', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Teacher Preparation Program reporting requirements under 20 U.S.C. §§ 1022-1022h mandate comprehensive annual reporting by institutions of higher education that offer teacher preparation programs. This federal regulation, implemented through 34 CFR Part 686, establishes accountability standards for educator preparation programs and ensures transparency in program performance.

Legal Authority: These requirements derive from the Higher Education Act of 1965, as reauthorized, specifically Title II Part A (20 U.S.C. § 1022a through 1022h). The Department of Education''s implementing regulations at 34 CFR 686.10 specify detailed reporting procedures and content requirements.

Core Reporting Requirements: Institutions must submit annual institutional report cards containing: (1) achievement of annual goals for increasing teachers in shortage areas, including specific numerical targets and actual placement data; (2) detailed improvement strategies and performance enhancement measures; (3) comprehensive program information including admissions criteria, GPA requirements, clinical experience hours per 20 U.S.C. § 1022c(a)(3); (4) state teacher licensing examination pass rates, average scores, and comparative state performance data; (7) current accreditation status from recognized accrediting bodies; (8) any state low-performing program designations under 20 U.S.C. § 1022e; (9) technology integration training activities and curriculum components; and (10) special education preparation activities addressing students with disabilities instruction.

Scope and Applicability: All institutions of higher education offering teacher preparation programs leading to initial teacher certification must comply, including public universities, private colleges, and alternative certification programs receiving federal funding under Title IV of the Higher Education Act.

Enforcement and Penalties: Non-compliance may result in loss of federal funding eligibility, program closure orders, or designation as low-performing under 20 U.S.C. § 1022f. The Secretary of Education may impose corrective action plans and enhanced monitoring requirements.

Recent Updates: The 2022 Department of Education guidance clarified technology integration reporting standards and expanded disability preparation documentation requirements, effective for reports submitted after October 2022.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:08.52128', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-069', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

 	There is an exception for undue hardship and for loans that became due more than seven years before the filing of the petition.', '', 'Finance', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:08.55938', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Bankruptcy Abuse Prevention and Consumer Protection Act of 2005 (BAPCPA), codified at 11 U.S.C. §§ 101-1532 and enacted as Public Law No. 109-8, fundamentally altered the dischargeability of educational debt in bankruptcy proceedings. Under 11 U.S.C. § 523(a)(8), student loans obtained from government entities, nonprofit institutions of higher education, or private lenders for qualified educational expenses are presumptively non-dischargeable in both Chapter 7 and Chapter 13 bankruptcy proceedings.

The Act applies to all federally-funded student loans, including Direct Loans, FFEL Program loans, Perkins Loans, and PLUS loans, as well as private educational loans meeting the statutory definition under 11 U.S.C. § 101(25). Higher education institutions must understand that loans they originate or guarantee fall under this protection, significantly impacting their financial aid counseling obligations under 34 CFR § 685.304.

The primary exception exists under the ''undue hardship'' standard, typically evaluated using the three-prong Brunner test: (1) the debtor cannot maintain a minimal standard of living while repaying loans; (2) additional circumstances suggest the hardship will persist; and (3) the debtor has made good faith efforts to repay. Courts apply this standard restrictively, with fewer than 1% of debtors successfully discharging student loans.

Under 11 U.S.C. § 523(a)(8)(A), loans from governmental units or nonprofit institutions of higher education maintaining tax-exempt status under 26 U.S.C. § 501(c)(3) receive absolute protection. The seven-year discharge provision was eliminated by BAPCPA, closing a previous avenue for discharge.

Institutions must implement proper loan documentation procedures and maintain accurate records supporting the educational purpose of disbursed funds. The Department of Education, through 34 CFR Part 682, requires institutions to certify loan eligibility and maintain comprehensive borrower records.

Enforcement occurs through bankruptcy court proceedings where creditors, including institutions, may object to discharge attempts. Institutions should monitor bankruptcy filings affecting their loan portfolios and engage qualified bankruptcy counsel when borrowers seek discharge.

Recent interpretations have clarified that loans for bar exam preparation courses and continuing education may qualify for protection if meeting statutory educational expense definitions under 11 U.S.C. § 101(25). The Supreme Court''s decision in Lamar, Archer & Cofrin, LLP v. Appling (2014) reinforced broad application of non-dischargeability provisions.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:08.55938', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-149', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

 	Prohibits price discrimination; conditioning sales on exclusive dealing; mergers and acquisitions when they may substantially reduce competition; and serving on the board of directors for two competing companies.', '', 'Finance', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:08.592019', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Clayton Antitrust Act of 1914, codified at 15 U.S.C. §§ 12-27, serves as a critical supplement to the Sherman Act, establishing comprehensive antitrust provisions that significantly impact higher education institutions'' business operations, procurement practices, and institutional governance. Originally enacted to address specific anti-competitive practices not adequately covered by existing antitrust legislation, the Clayton Act prohibits four primary categories of conduct that can substantially reduce competition.

Section 2 of the Clayton Act (15 U.S.C. § 13) prohibits price discrimination, preventing universities from engaging in discriminatory pricing practices when purchasing goods or services. This provision, enhanced by the Robinson-Patman Act amendments, requires higher education institutions to ensure their procurement processes do not involve receiving preferential pricing that could harm competition among suppliers. Universities must maintain documentation demonstrating that any price differentials are based on legitimate cost justifications or competitive bidding processes.

Section 3 (15 U.S.C. § 14) addresses exclusive dealing arrangements and tying agreements, prohibiting universities from conditioning purchases on exclusive supplier relationships when such arrangements may substantially lessen competition. This impacts technology contracts, food service agreements, bookstore operations, and other vendor relationships. Institutions must carefully structure contracts to avoid creating illegal market monopolies while maintaining operational efficiency.

Section 7 (15 U.S.C. § 18) regulates mergers and acquisitions, requiring universities considering institutional mergers, asset acquisitions, or joint ventures to evaluate potential anti-competitive effects. The Hart-Scott-Rodino Antitrust Improvements Act of 1976 (15 U.S.C. § 18a) mandates pre-merger notification to federal antitrust agencies for transactions exceeding specified thresholds, currently $101 million as adjusted annually.

Section 8 (15 U.S.C. § 19) prohibits interlocking directorates, preventing individuals from simultaneously serving on boards of competing institutions or companies. This provision directly impacts university governance, requiring institutions to screen board members, trustees, and senior administrators for potential conflicts involving competing educational institutions or related businesses.

Enforcement mechanisms include civil penalties up to $46,517 per violation as adjusted under the Federal Civil Penalties Inflation Adjustment Act (28 U.S.C. § 2461 note). The Federal Trade Commission and Department of Justice Antitrust Division share enforcement authority, with private parties also empowered to seek treble damages under 15 U.S.C. § 15. Universities face significant exposure given their substantial procurement volumes, extensive vendor relationships, and complex governance structures.

Recent enforcement trends emphasize digital marketplace competition, particularly relevant for universities'' technology procurement and online education platforms. The 2021 Executive Order on Promoting Competition in the American Economy specifically addresses educational market competition, signaling increased scrutiny of higher education practices.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:08.592019', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-150', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

 	Note that when using a third party consumer reporting agency to request motor vehicle record checks for employment purposes, the FCRA should be followed, and notice given to the applicant or employee.', '', 'Human Resources', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:08.622367', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Fair Credit Reporting Act (FCRA), codified at 15 U.S.C. §§ 1681-1681v, is a federal statute that regulates the collection, dissemination, and use of consumer information, including consumer credit information and employment background checks. For higher education institutions, FCRA compliance is critical when conducting background checks on prospective employees, including faculty, staff, and student employees.

Under 15 U.S.C. § 1681b(b)(2)(A), colleges and universities must provide clear and conspicuous written disclosure to applicants before obtaining any consumer report for employment purposes. This disclosure must be in a document that consists solely of the disclosure and cannot be included within employment applications or other materials. The institution must also obtain written authorization from the applicant as specified in 15 U.S.C. § 1681b(b)(2)(B).

When adverse employment action is contemplated based on information in a consumer report, institutions must comply with the pre-adverse action requirements under 15 U.S.C. § 1681b(b)(3)(A). This includes providing the applicant with a copy of the consumer report and a summary of consumer rights under FCRA. After taking adverse action, institutions must provide notice that includes the name and contact information of the consumer reporting agency, a statement that the agency did not make the decision, and notification of the applicant''s right to dispute the accuracy of the report under 15 U.S.C. § 1681m(a).

Higher education institutions must certify to consumer reporting agencies that they will comply with FCRA requirements and will not use information in violation of equal employment opportunity laws, as mandated by 15 U.S.C. § 1681b(b)(1). The Federal Trade Commission (FTC) enforces FCRA compliance and can impose civil penalties up to $4,304 per violation under 15 U.S.C. § 1681s(a)(2)(A). Private parties may also bring civil actions for willful or negligent non-compliance, with damages ranging from $100-$1,000 per violation plus actual damages and attorney fees under 15 U.S.C. § 1681n.

Recent amendments through the Fair and Accurate Credit Transactions Act have enhanced requirements for disposal of consumer information and identity theft prevention, requiring institutions to implement reasonable measures to protect against unauthorized access to consumer reports.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:08.622367', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-210', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

 	An employee who is in employment for wages which are subject to taxes under the Federal Insurance Contributions Act (FICA) or which are subject to the withholding of income tax from wages must apply for a social security number by filing SSA Form SS-5.

 	An employer must pay social security taxes on employees, but an exemption exists for most students who are also working for the university and for clergy whose services are performed in the exercise of their ministry.', '', 'Human Resources', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:08.650719', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Federal Insurance Contributions Act (FICA), codified at 26 U.S.C. §§ 3101-3128, establishes the framework for Social Security and Medicare taxation for employers and employees, including higher education institutions. Under 26 U.S.C. § 3121(b)(10), FICA provides a critical exemption for student employees that significantly impacts university payroll operations and tax compliance.

The student exemption under 26 U.S.C. § 3121(b)(10) exempts services performed by students who are enrolled and regularly attending classes at the employing educational institution from FICA taxation. This exemption applies to both the employer''s 7.65% contribution and the employee''s 7.65% withholding for Social Security (6.2%) and Medicare (1.45%) taxes. The Internal Revenue Service interprets ''regularly attending classes'' through Revenue Ruling 2005-11, which requires students to carry a course load that is at least half of the full-time equivalent as determined by the institution.

Under 26 U.S.C. § 3111, universities must pay the employer portion of FICA taxes for all non-exempt employees. The exemption extends beyond traditional undergraduate students to include graduate students, postdoctoral fellows, and continuing education students who meet enrollment criteria. However, per 26 C.F.R. § 31.3121(b)(10)-2, the exemption terminates immediately when students cease enrollment or drop below half-time status.

Additionally, 26 U.S.C. § 3121(b)(8)(A) provides an exemption for clergy members performing services in the exercise of their ministry, including university chaplains and theology faculty with ministerial duties. This exemption requires careful documentation of the religious nature of services performed.

Universities must maintain detailed payroll records per 26 C.F.R. § 31.6001-1, documenting student enrollment status, course loads, and employment periods to substantiate exemption claims. The IRS may impose penalties under 26 U.S.C. § 6656 for failure to deposit required FICA taxes, with penalties ranging from 2% to 15% of unpaid taxes depending on the delay period. Additional penalties under 26 U.S.C. § 6721 apply for incorrect Form W-2 reporting, with fines up to $280 per form.

Recent guidance in Notice 2013-54 clarified that student loan forgiveness programs may trigger FICA liability for amounts forgiven as compensation. Universities must also comply with the Affordable Care Act''s additional Medicare tax of 0.9% under 26 U.S.C. § 3101(b)(2) for employees earning over $200,000 annually, with no student exemption available for this additional tax.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:08.650719', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-175', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

 	Provides for payments of unemployment compensation to workers who have lost their jobs.

 	If an employer’s FUTA tax is more than $500 for the calendar year, the employer must deposit at least one quarterly payment.  If not, alternate rules apply. See IRS Publication: Topic Number 759 - Form 940 – Employer''s Annual Federal Unemployment (FUTA) Tax Return – Filing and Deposit Requirements', '', 'Finance', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:08.698054', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Federal Unemployment Tax Act (FUTA), codified at 26 U.S.C. §§ 3301-3311, establishes a federal-state unemployment insurance system that requires employers, including higher education institutions, to pay federal unemployment taxes to fund unemployment compensation for displaced workers. FUTA operates in conjunction with state unemployment insurance programs to provide temporary financial assistance to eligible unemployed individuals.

Higher education institutions are subject to FUTA requirements as employers when they meet specific criteria outlined in 26 U.S.C. § 3306(c). Universities and colleges must pay FUTA tax at a rate of 6.0% on the first $7,000 of wages paid to each employee during the calendar year, as specified in 26 U.S.C. § 3301. However, employers may claim a credit of up to 5.4% against their FUTA tax liability for contributions paid to state unemployment funds under 26 U.S.C. § 3302, effectively reducing the net FUTA tax rate to 0.6% for compliant employers.

Under 26 U.S.C. § 3304, institutions must ensure their state unemployment compensation law meets federal conformity requirements to maintain the tax credit. Special provisions apply to educational institutions under 26 U.S.C. § 3304(a)(6), which allows states to deny benefits to individuals between academic years or terms if they have reasonable assurance of returning to work.

FUTA obligations extend to various categories of higher education employees, including faculty, staff, graduate assistants, and student workers, subject to specific exemptions under 26 U.S.C. § 3306(c)(10) for students enrolled and regularly attending classes. The Internal Revenue Service enforces FUTA compliance through 26 CFR Part 31, with penalties for non-compliance including interest charges, failure-to-deposit penalties under 26 U.S.C. § 6656, and failure-to-file penalties under 26 U.S.C. § 6651.

Institutions must maintain detailed payroll records and calculate FUTA tax liability quarterly, depositing taxes when the cumulative liability exceeds $500 in any quarter, as required by 26 CFR § 31.6302-1. The Protecting Americans from Tax Hikes (PATH) Act of 2015 enhanced enforcement mechanisms and reporting requirements, while recent IRS guidance clarifies application to remote work arrangements and multi-state operations common in higher education.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:08.698054', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-151', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

Under 20 U.S.C. § 1011f(a)(1), covered institutions include any institution that receives federal financial assistance under Title IV of the Higher Education Act. The statutory definition of "foreign source" encompasses foreign governments, foreign legal entities, individuals who are not U.S. citizens or permanent residents, and any entity owned or controlled by foreign interests as specified in 20 U.S.C. § 1011f(h)(1).

Reporting obligations under 34 CFR § 668.14(b)(20) require institutions to submit disclosure reports to the Department of Education''s Office of Postsecondary Education within specified timeframes. These reports must detail the aggregate dollar amount, country of origin, date of receipt, and restricted or conditional nature of gifts exceeding the threshold. For contracts, institutions must disclose the country and nature of services performed, along with compensation details.

The Department of Education maintains enforcement authority under 20 U.S.C. § 1011f(d), including the power to conduct investigations, impose civil penalties up to $1,094,785 per violation as adjusted for inflation under 34 CFR § 668.83, and potentially terminate federal funding eligibility for non-compliance. Recent enforcement actions demonstrate the Department''s commitment to rigorous oversight, with institutions facing substantial penalties for incomplete or untimely disclosures.

Amendments effective January 2020 expanded reporting requirements to include conditional gifts and clarified aggregation methodologies for related foreign entities. The Department issued updated guidance in Federal Register Notice 85 FR 59916, emphasizing enhanced due diligence requirements for identifying beneficial ownership of foreign sources and implementing comprehensive internal controls for tracking reportable transactions.

Institutions must establish robust compliance frameworks incorporating regular training for development officers, financial personnel, and senior administrators involved in foreign relationship management. The regulation''s broad scope encompasses research collaborations, student exchange programs, facility naming rights, endowment contributions, and consulting arrangements that meet the monetary threshold established in 20 U.S.C. § 1011f(a)(2).', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:08.726971', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-152', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

 	Institutions must also provide payors with a statement that includes the tax information furnished to the IRS.', '', 'Finance', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:08.757392', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '26 U.S.C. § 6050S establishes mandatory information reporting requirements for eligible educational institutions regarding qualified tuition payments and student loan interest received. This regulation, implemented through Treasury Regulation 1.6050S-1, requires institutions to file annual information returns with the Internal Revenue Service and furnish corresponding statements to payors. The statutory framework originated from the Taxpayer Relief Act of 1997 and has been amended by subsequent legislation including the American Opportunity Tax Credit provisions under 26 U.S.C. § 25A.

Eligible educational institutions, defined under 26 U.S.C. § 25A(f)(2) as institutions eligible to participate in federal student aid programs under Title IV of the Higher Education Act (20 U.S.C. § 1088), must report qualified tuition and related expenses on Form 1098-T. Qualified expenses include tuition, fees required for enrollment, and course materials required for enrollment, but exclude room and board, medical expenses, transportation, and similar personal expenses as specified in 26 U.S.C. § 25A(f)(1).

Institutions must report aggregate payments received during the calendar year for qualified tuition and related expenses, scholarships and grants processed by the institution, adjustments to prior year amounts, and whether the student was enrolled at least half-time or was a graduate student. The reporting threshold requires institutions to file Form 1098-T for any student who paid qualified tuition and related expenses during the calendar year, with limited exceptions for nonresident aliens unless requested by the student.

For student loan interest reporting, institutions that receive interest payments of $600 or more during the calendar year must file Form 1098-E and furnish statements to payors under 26 U.S.C. § 6050S(e). This applies to qualified student loans as defined in 26 U.S.C. § 221(d), covering loans incurred solely to pay qualified education expenses.

Compliance deadlines mandate filing information returns with the IRS by February 28 (or March 31 if filing electronically) following the calendar year for which reporting is required. Payee statements must be furnished by January 31 following the calendar year. Institutions filing 250 or more information returns annually must file electronically pursuant to 26 U.S.C. § 6011(e).

Penalty provisions under 26 U.S.C. § 6721 impose fines for failure to file correct information returns, currently $280 per return with maximum annual penalties reaching $3.426 million for large institutions. Failure to furnish correct payee statements incurs penalties under 26 U.S.C. § 6722, with similar monetary amounts. Intentional disregard of filing requirements results in minimum penalties of $580 per return with no maximum limitation.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:08.757392', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-192', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

Key provisions under 15 U.S.C. § 1693b require universities to provide clear disclosures before students engage in EFT services, including fee schedules, liability limits, and dispute resolution procedures. Higher education institutions must comply with 12 CFR § 1005.15, which specifically addresses payroll card accounts that parallel student account structures. Universities offering campus card programs linked to bank accounts must ensure compliance with periodic statement requirements under 12 CFR § 1005.9, providing monthly statements or alternative access methods.

Error resolution procedures under 15 U.S.C. § 1693f mandate that institutions investigate disputed transactions within specific timeframes: preliminary investigations must commence within 10 business days, with provisional credit required if investigations extend beyond this period. Universities must complete investigations within 45 days for most transactions, or 90 days for point-of-sale debit card transactions or foreign-initiated transfers.

Consumer liability limitations under 15 U.S.C. § 1693g restrict student liability to $50 for unauthorized transfers if reported within two business days, increasing to $500 if reported between two and 60 days. Failure to report within 60 days may result in unlimited liability for subsequent unauthorized transfers. Universities must establish robust notification systems ensuring students receive immediate access to account information.

Enforcement mechanisms include CFPB examination authority under 12 U.S.C. § 5514, with civil penalties up to $1 million per day for violations under 12 U.S.C. § 5565. Recent amendments through the 2019 prepaid card rule under 12 CFR § 1005.20 expanded coverage to include many campus card programs, requiring enhanced fee disclosures and account access provisions. Universities must conduct annual compliance reviews and maintain documentation demonstrating adherence to all disclosure, error resolution, and consumer protection requirements.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:08.983293', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-155', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

 	Does not apply to public entities but may represent best practices.

 	18 U.S. Code § 1519 makes destruction, alteration, or falsification of records in federal investigations, bankruptcy, or any matter within the jurisdiction of a federal department or agency a crime punishable by fines and up to 20 years imprisonment, and applies to all individuals, not just publicly traded corporations.

 	Bars independent auditors from providing certain non-audit services to the client institution.', '', 'Finance', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:09.015265', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Sarbanes-Oxley Act of 2002 (SOX), enacted as Public Law No. 107-204, was designed to enhance corporate accountability and financial transparency following major corporate scandals. While primarily targeting publicly traded companies, SOX contains provisions with significant implications for higher education institutions, particularly private universities with substantial endowments and public institutions managing federal funds.

Under 15 U.S.C. § 7201 et seq., SOX established the Public Company Accounting Oversight Board (PCAOB) and mandated enhanced financial reporting standards. For higher education, the most critical provision is 18 U.S.C. § 1519, which criminalizes the destruction, alteration, or falsification of records in federal investigations or matters within federal agency jurisdiction. This statute applies to all entities, including universities receiving federal funding through Title IV programs, research grants, or other federal assistance.

Section 802 of SOX, codified at 18 U.S.C. § 1519, imposes severe penalties including fines and up to 20 years imprisonment for knowingly altering, destroying, mutilating, concealing, or falsifying documents with intent to impede federal investigations. Universities must maintain comprehensive document retention policies covering financial records, research data, student files, and correspondence related to federal compliance matters.

SOX Section 201, codified at 15 U.S.C. § 78j-1, prohibits auditing firms from providing certain non-audit services to clients, including bookkeeping, financial system design, appraisal services, actuarial services, internal audit outsourcing, management functions, human resources, broker-dealer services, legal services, and expert services unrelated to auditing. Universities engaging external auditors must ensure strict separation between audit and consulting services to maintain auditor independence.

While not legally required to comply with SOX corporate governance provisions, many universities adopt SOX best practices including establishing audit committees with independent oversight, implementing internal controls over financial reporting similar to Section 404 requirements (15 U.S.C. § 7262), and maintaining whistleblower protection policies aligned with Section 806 provisions (18 U.S.C. § 1514A).

Enforcement mechanisms include criminal prosecution by the Department of Justice under 18 U.S.C. § 1519 and potential civil penalties through federal agency actions. Universities may face additional consequences including suspension or termination of federal funding, exclusion from federal programs, and civil liability under related statutes. The statute of limitations for criminal violations is generally five years under 18 U.S.C. § 3282, though ongoing conspiracies may extend this period.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:09.015265', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-156', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

 	This law has been applied to colleges and universities in a number of instances regarding purchases.

 	The Department of Justice interpreted this law to mean that financial aid awards must be established independently and not in concert with other institutions.

 	May also apply to establishing prices for items in a bookstore based on collaboration with a wholesaler or another retailer.

 	Section 568 - known as the antitrust exemption - allows colleges and universities to collaborate on a common formula for determining a student''s financial need.', '', 'Finance', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:09.043923', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Sherman Antitrust Act of 1890, codified at 15 U.S.C. §§ 1-7, represents foundational federal legislation prohibiting anticompetitive business practices that restrain trade or commerce. Section 1 of the Act (15 U.S.C. § 1) specifically prohibits "every contract, combination in the form of trust or otherwise, or conspiracy, in restraint of trade or commerce among the several States, or with foreign nations."

For higher education institutions, the Sherman Act has particular significance in several operational areas. The Department of Justice has consistently interpreted the Act to require that financial aid determinations must be made independently by each institution without coordination or agreement with competing institutions. This interpretation stems from the principle that financial aid represents price competition in the higher education marketplace, and coordination could constitute illegal price-fixing under 15 U.S.C. § 1.

However, Congress created a limited antitrust exemption specifically for higher education through Section 568 of the Improving America''s Schools Act of 1994 (15 U.S.C. § 1 note). This exemption permits institutions to collaborate on methodologies for determining student financial need, provided they meet specific criteria including need-blind admissions practices and commitment to meet full demonstrated financial need. The exemption has been reauthorized multiple times, most recently extended through September 30, 2022.

The Act''s application extends beyond financial aid to institutional purchasing decisions. Universities engaging in group purchasing organizations or collaborative procurement must ensure such arrangements do not constitute unlawful restraints on trade. Similarly, bookstore operations face scrutiny when coordinating pricing with wholesalers or other retailers, as such coordination may violate 15 U.S.C. § 1 if it unreasonably restrains competition.

Enforcement mechanisms include both criminal and civil penalties under 15 U.S.C. § 2 and § 4. Criminal violations can result in fines up to $100 million for corporations and up to $1 million and 10 years imprisonment for individuals under 15 U.S.C. § 1. Civil enforcement allows for treble damages and injunctive relief. The Federal Trade Commission and Department of Justice share enforcement authority, with private parties also able to bring civil actions under 15 U.S.C. § 15.

Recent enforcement actions have targeted graduate student stipend coordination and faculty salary discussions among competing institutions. The DOJ has emphasized that any agreement affecting price, including non-monetary benefits, may constitute a Sherman Act violation regardless of benevolent intent.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:09.043923', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-157', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

 	To avoid penalty for reporting incorrect SSN on W-2 forms, transmit data file electronically to the Social Security Administration (SSA). Report all employees hired in the specific quarter listing name (as it appears in HR System), SSN, sex, and date of birth. After information is queried against SSA''s database, mismatches are sent back for resolution.', '', 'Finance', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:09.072436', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Social Security Act, codified at 42 U.S.C. §§ 301-1397mm, establishes the framework for social security taxation and reporting obligations that significantly impact higher education institutions as employers. Under 26 U.S.C. § 3101 and § 3111, both employers and employees must pay Federal Insurance Contributions Act (FICA) taxes at 6.2% for Social Security and 1.45% for Medicare on wages up to the annual wage base ($160,200 for 2023).

Higher education institutions benefit from specific exemptions under 26 U.S.C. § 3121(b)(10) and implementing regulations at 26 CFR § 31.3121(b)(10)-2. Students enrolled at least half-time and employed by the same institution are exempt from FICA taxes when their primary relationship is educational rather than employment-focused. This exemption applies to undergraduate students, graduate students in degree programs, and professional students. The exemption terminates when the educational relationship ends, requiring immediate implementation of FICA withholding.

Clergy members performing services in the exercise of their ministry are exempt under 26 U.S.C. § 3121(b)(8)(A), though they remain subject to Self-Employment Contributions Act (SECA) taxes under 26 U.S.C. § 1402. Professional employees, as defined in 26 CFR § 31.3121(b)(10)-2(d), may qualify for exemptions when employed in their capacity as enrolled students.

Compliance requires universities to maintain accurate Social Security Number (SSN) records under 26 U.S.C. § 6051 and 26 CFR § 31.6051-1. The Social Security Administration Number Verification Service (SSNVS) mandate requires electronic verification of employee SSNs to prevent penalties under 26 U.S.C. § 6721, which imposes fines up to $280 per incorrect Form W-2 (maximum $3.4 million annually). Universities must submit quarterly new hire reports to state directories under 42 U.S.C. § 653a within 20 days of hire, including employee name, SSN, address, and employment date.

Enforcement mechanisms include IRS audits, penalty assessments, and potential criminal sanctions under 26 U.S.C. § 7201 for willful evasion. The IRS may impose trust fund recovery penalties under 26 U.S.C. § 6672 against responsible university officials who willfully fail to collect or remit required taxes. Recent updates include increased penalty amounts under the Protecting Americans from Tax Hikes Act and enhanced electronic filing requirements for institutions with 10+ employees.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:09.072436', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-158', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

 	Have at least 500 students

 	Have more than 50% of students located in the U.S.

 	Have assets valued at the close of the preceding tax year of at least $500,000 per full-time student.', '', 'Finance', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:09.101343', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Tax Cuts and Jobs Act of 2017 (TCJA) established Section 4968 of the Internal Revenue Code (26 U.S.C. § 4968), imposing a 1.4 percent excise tax on the net investment income of certain private colleges and universities. This federal regulation represents the first time in U.S. history that higher education endowments have been subject to federal taxation.

Legal Authority: The excise tax is codified under 26 U.S.C. § 4968 and implemented through Treasury Regulations 26 CFR § 53.4968-1 through 53.4968-3. The Internal Revenue Service (IRS) has enforcement authority under 26 U.S.C. § 7805.

Applicability Requirements: Private educational institutions are subject to this tax if they meet three specific criteria simultaneously: (1) at least 500 tuition-paying students during the preceding taxable year, determined under 26 CFR § 53.4968-1(b); (2) more than 50 percent of students are located in the United States, as defined in 26 CFR § 53.4968-1(c); and (3) aggregate fair market value of assets at year-end (other than those used directly in educational purposes) equals at least $500,000 per full-time student, calculated under 26 CFR § 53.4968-1(d).

Net Investment Income Definition: Under 26 CFR § 53.4968-2, taxable net investment income includes dividends, interest, rents, royalties, capital gains, and similar amounts, minus directly connected deductions. Income from assets used directly in the institution''s educational purpose is excluded.

Compliance and Reporting: Affected institutions must file Form 4720 (Return of Certain Excise Taxes) annually by the 15th day of the 5th month following the tax year end, as specified in 26 U.S.C. § 6033(a). The tax must be paid with the return filing.

Enforcement and Penalties: The IRS enforces compliance through standard tax collection procedures under 26 U.S.C. § 6651. Failure to file results in penalties of 5 percent per month up to 25 percent of unpaid tax. Failure to pay incurs 0.5 percent monthly penalties under 26 U.S.C. § 6651(a)(2).

Recent Developments: Treasury Regulations finalized in 2020 clarified asset valuation methods and student counting procedures. The COVID-19 pandemic raised questions about student location requirements for remote learning, addressed through IRS guidance permitting temporary location changes without affecting the 50 percent threshold calculation.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:09.101343', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-160', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

 	Loans made, insured or guaranteed pursuant to programs authorized by Title IV are exempt.', '', 'Finance', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:09.129364', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Truth in Lending Act (TILA), codified at 15 U.S.C. §§ 1601-1693r, is a federal consumer protection law that mandates clear disclosure of credit terms and costs to borrowers. For higher education institutions, TILA''s application is significantly limited by specific exemptions for federal student aid programs. Under 15 U.S.C. § 1603(1) and implementing regulations at 12 CFR § 1026.3(a)(1), loans made, insured, or guaranteed pursuant to Title IV of the Higher Education Act are exempt from TILA requirements. This exemption encompasses Federal Direct Loans, Federal Perkins Loans, Federal Family Education Loans (FFEL), and PLUS loans. However, institutions must comply with TILA for non-federal educational credit products, including institutional payment plans exceeding four installments under 12 CFR § 1026.2(a)(20), private education loans from third-party lenders that don''t qualify for exemptions, and emergency or bridge loans offered directly by the institution. TILA requires disclosure of Annual Percentage Rate (APR), finance charges, total amount financed, total of payments, and payment schedule within specific timeframes outlined in 12 CFR § 1026.18. For open-end credit plans, periodic statements must include information specified in 12 CFR § 1026.7. Enforcement is primarily conducted by the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau (CFPB) under 12 U.S.C. § 5514, with civil penalties up to $1,000,000 per day for knowing violations per 12 U.S.C. § 5565(c). The CFPB has supervisory authority over institutions with over $10 billion in assets. Private rights of action exist under 15 U.S.C. § 1640, allowing borrowers to recover actual damages plus statutory damages up to $4,000 for individual actions. Recent amendments through the Dodd-Frank Act transferred rulemaking authority from the Federal Reserve to the CFPB in 2011, while the Economic Growth, Regulatory Relief, and Consumer Protection Act of 2018 modified certain disclosure requirements for qualified mortgages. Institutions must maintain compliance documentation for two years minimum under 12 CFR § 1026.25, and ensure staff training on TILA requirements for any covered credit products. Non-compliance risks include regulatory sanctions, civil monetary penalties, and private litigation exposure.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:09.129364', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-161', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

 	Department of Education exceptions to the Uniform Guidance are available here.', '', 'Finance', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:09.15736', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Uniform Administrative Requirements, Cost Principles, and Audit Requirements for Federal Awards, codified at 2 CFR Part 200, represents the most comprehensive reform of federal grant administration in decades. This regulation, effective December 26, 2014, consolidates eight previous Office of Management and Budget (OMB) circulars into a single, streamlined framework governing all federal financial assistance programs.

Legal Authority: The Uniform Guidance derives its authority from 31 U.S.C. § 503 and implements requirements across all federal agencies, with specific provisions for research universities under Subpart E - Cost Principles (2 CFR §§ 200.400-200.475) and Subpart F - Audit Requirements (2 CFR §§ 200.500-200.521). The Department of Education has issued limited exceptions under 2 CFR Part 3474.

Key Provisions: Universities must establish robust financial management systems meeting standards in 2 CFR § 200.302, including separation of duties, accurate financial reporting, and effective internal controls. Cost accounting requirements under 2 CFR § 200.403 mandate that all costs be necessary, reasonable, allocable, and properly documented. The regulation introduces significant changes including: (1) uniform 10% de minimis indirect cost rate option (2 CFR § 200.414(f)); (2) enhanced conflict of interest disclosure requirements (2 CFR § 200.112); (3) revised equipment thresholds at $5,000 (2 CFR § 200.33); and (4) mandatory disclosure of violations of federal criminal law (2 CFR § 200.113).

Scope and Applicability: All higher education institutions receiving federal awards exceeding $25,000 must comply, with enhanced audit requirements under 2 CFR Part 200 Subpart F for institutions expending $750,000 or more annually. Research universities face particular scrutiny regarding F&A cost proposals, time and effort reporting, and subaward monitoring under 2 CFR §§ 200.330-200.332.

Enforcement: Non-compliance may result in award suspension, termination, withholding of payments, or debarment under 2 CFR §§ 200.338-200.342. The regulation establishes uniform audit requirements with findings potentially triggering corrective action plans and enhanced federal oversight.

Recent Updates: OMB issued clarifying guidance in 2020 regarding COVID-19 flexibility measures and updated procurement standards. The regulation continues to evolve through federal agency implementation and periodic OMB memoranda addressing emerging compliance issues.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:09.15736', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-162', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

Under 26 U.S.C. § 512, unrelated business taxable income (UBTI) consists of gross income from any unrelated trade or business regularly carried on by the organization, less allowable deductions directly connected to such business. The Internal Revenue Code defines unrelated trade or business as any trade or business not substantially related to the organization''s exempt purpose, except that it provides funds for such purpose. For universities, this includes activities like operating commercial businesses, rental of facilities to outside parties for non-educational purposes, and advertising revenue from publications.

Higher education institutions must evaluate three criteria under 26 U.S.C. § 513: (1) the activity constitutes a trade or business, (2) it is regularly carried on with frequency and continuity comparable to commercial enterprises, and (3) it is not substantially related to the institution''s educational mission. Treasury Regulation § 1.513-1 clarifies that substantially related means the activity contributes importantly to accomplishing the organization''s exempt purposes, beyond merely providing funds.

Key exemptions under 26 U.S.C. § 512(b) include passive income such as dividends, interest, royalties, and rents from real property, volunteer-conducted activities, businesses carried on primarily for member convenience, and activities where substantially all work is performed by volunteers. Educational institutions benefit from specific exceptions including qualified research activities under 26 U.S.C. § 512(b)(7)-(9).

Compliance requires filing Form 990-T annually if gross unrelated business income exceeds $1,000, as mandated by 26 U.S.C. § 6012(a)(6). The current corporate tax rate applies to net UBTI. Penalties for non-compliance include failure-to-file penalties under 26 U.S.C. § 6652(c)(1)(A) of $20 per day up to the lesser of $10,000 or 5% of gross receipts, plus potential loss of tax-exempt status for substantial commercial activities.

Recent amendments include the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act of 2017, which requires separate calculation of UBTI for each unrelated trade or business under 26 U.S.C. § 512(a)(6), and subjects certain fringe benefits to UBIT under 26 U.S.C. § 512(a)(7). The IRS has increased enforcement through detailed Form 990-T instructions and audit initiatives targeting higher education institutions with significant commercial activities.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:09.208393', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-163', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

1. Clearly define institutional mission and educational objectives with measurable outcomes
2. Maintain program quality standards across all instructional modalities including distance education
3. Ensure curriculum content, faculty qualifications, and institutional resources meet accreditor standards
4. Demonstrate student achievement outcomes that align with stated program objectives
5. Work with federally recognized accrediting agencies to maintain Title IV eligibility', 'Academic Programs', 'state', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:09.238326', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Higher Education Act''s Recognition of Accrediting Agency or Association provision, codified at 20 U.S.C. § 1099b(a)(4)(A), establishes critical standards that accrediting agencies must enforce to maintain federal recognition and enable institutions to participate in Title IV federal student aid programs. This regulation requires accrediting agencies to enforce standards that respect institutional missions, including religious missions, while ensuring educational program quality meets stated objectives throughout the accreditation period.

Under 20 U.S.C. § 1099b(a)(4)(A) and implementing regulations at 34 CFR § 602.16, accrediting agencies must demonstrate they enforce standards requiring institutions to clearly define their missions and educational objectives, then maintain program quality sufficient to achieve those objectives. This applies to all instructional modalities, including traditional classroom instruction, distance education, and correspondence programs.

The Department of Education evaluates accrediting agencies against these criteria during initial recognition proceedings under 34 CFR § 602.31 and periodic review cycles under 34 CFR § 602.32. Agencies must provide evidence their standards address curriculum content, faculty qualifications, student achievement outcomes, and institutional resources adequate to support program delivery across all offered modalities.

Key provisions include mission-based evaluation standards that allow institutions flexibility in defining educational objectives while requiring measurable outcomes. Accrediting agencies must enforce standards ensuring distance education and correspondence programs meet the same quality thresholds as traditional programs, addressing concerns about program integrity across delivery methods under 34 CFR § 602.17.

Enforcement mechanisms include Department of Education authority to limit, suspend, or terminate accrediting agency recognition under 34 CFR § 602.36 for failure to meet statutory requirements. Institutions accredited by non-recognized agencies lose Title IV eligibility, creating significant financial consequences. The Department conducts compliance reviews and may impose conditions on recognition requiring corrective actions within specified timeframes.

Recent regulatory updates through the 2020 accreditation regulations strengthened requirements for outcome-based standards and clarified agency obligations for distance education oversight, emphasizing student achievement measures and institutional capacity assessments.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:09.238326', NULL, true, NULL, 'PA', NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'state', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'PA-036', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

Under 20 U.S.C. § 1091c(a), eligible students include those who served in the Army, Navy, Air Force, Marine Corps, Coast Guard, commissioned corps of the Public Health Service, or National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration. The statute applies to both voluntary enlistment and involuntary service, including National Guard and Reserve activations exceeding 30 days.

The five-year service limitation, established in 20 U.S.C. § 1091c(a)(2), begins from the initial date of service and includes cumulative military service periods. However, this limitation extends beyond five years for servicemembers unable to return due to hospitalization or convalescence from service-connected injuries, or those serving beyond their initial obligation involuntarily.

Exclusions from readmission protection under 20 U.S.C. § 1091c(c) include students discharged under other than honorable conditions, including dishonorable discharge, bad conduct discharge, or dismissal by court-martial. Additionally, institutions may deny readmission when they determine it is not reasonably feasible to prepare the student to resume their program, considering technological or curricular changes during the absence period.

Institutions must readmit eligible servicemembers with the same academic status, enrollment status, and number of credit hours previously attained, as specified in 20 U.S.C. § 1091c(b)(1). This includes maintaining the same academic standing and progression toward degree completion. The institution must also provide the same academic catalog under which the student was previously enrolled, unless the student requests enrollment under current catalog provisions.

Application procedures require servicemembers to provide advance written or oral notice of military service, except when precluded by military necessity or otherwise impossible. Upon return, students must submit readmission applications within three years of service completion, as mandated by 20 U.S.C. § 1091c(a)(2)(A).

Tuition and fee obligations under this regulation require institutions to assess the same rates that would have been charged for the academic period during which the student served, adjusted for any generally applicable increases. The Department of Education enforces compliance through Title IV program reviews and may impose sanctions including fine, limitation, suspension, or termination of federal student aid eligibility for non-compliant institutions.

Recent interpretative guidance from the Department of Education clarifies that online and distance education programs fall within the statute''s scope, and institutions must accommodate reasonable scheduling conflicts arising from continued military obligations, including Reserve and National Guard duties.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:09.266473', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-153', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

1. Ensure physical accessibility of all facilities and programs
2. Provide auxiliary aids and services for effective communication
3. Modify policies and procedures to accommodate disabilities
4. Maintain nondiscriminatory practices in all government services
5. Establish grievance procedures for disability-related complaints', 'Athletics', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:09.306819', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Equity in Athletics Disclosure Act (EADA), codified at 20 U.S.C. § 1092(g), mandates comprehensive annual reporting of athletic program data by coeducational institutions of higher education that participate in Title IV federal student aid programs and maintain intercollegiate athletics programs. Implemented through 34 CFR § 668.41, this federal transparency requirement serves to promote gender equity awareness in collegiate athletics by making comparative data publicly available.

The statute requires institutions to report detailed information disaggregated by men''s and women''s teams, including: (1) the number of male and female students participating in intercollegiate athletics; (2) operating expenses and revenues attributable to men''s and women''s teams; (3) the number, gender, and employment status of athletic trainers and team physicians; (4) coaching staff compensation and demographics; and (5) recruitment expenses by gender. Under 20 U.S.C. § 1092(g)(4), institutions must also report athletically-related student aid awarded to male and female athletes.

Applicability extends to all Title IV-eligible institutions offering intercollegiate athletic programs, regardless of NCAA membership or division status. The Department of Education''s implementing regulations at 34 CFR § 668.41(c) specify that institutions must compile data for each sport in which they sponsor teams and maintain detailed records supporting their annual submissions.

Compliance obligations include submitting the EADA report electronically through the Department''s web-based survey system by October 15 annually, covering the preceding academic year''s data. The reported information becomes publicly accessible through the Department''s Equity in Athletics Data Analysis website, enabling stakeholders to compare institutional athletic program investments across gender lines.

Enforcement mechanisms include potential Title IV funding penalties for non-compliance, administrative sanctions, and public disclosure of institutional non-participation. Recent regulatory updates have clarified reporting requirements for emerging sports and standardized expense categorization methodologies. Institutions failing to submit complete, accurate reports within required timeframes may face federal compliance reviews and corrective action requirements under general Title IV enforcement authorities at 34 CFR Part 668, Subpart G.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:09.306819', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-017', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

