These hazardous chemicals include those regulated under 29 CFR 1910 subpart Z, and those defined under the Hazard Communication Standard, 29 CFR 1910.1200', '', 'Environmental Health & Safety', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:12.244544', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Occupational Safety and Health Administration''s Occupational Exposure to Hazardous Chemicals in Laboratories Standard, codified at 29 CFR § 1910.1450, establishes comprehensive safety requirements for laboratory operations in higher education institutions. This regulation, commonly known as the Laboratory Standard, represents a performance-based approach to chemical safety that recognizes the unique nature of laboratory work compared to industrial chemical operations. The standard applies specifically to laboratories where hazardous chemicals are used in relatively small quantities on a nonproduction basis, where multiple chemical procedures are conducted, and where protective laboratory practices and equipment are employed to minimize employee exposure to hazardous chemicals.

Higher education institutions must understand that this regulation covers all laboratory employees, including faculty, staff, graduate students, undergraduate students, and any other personnel who work with hazardous chemicals in laboratory settings. The definition of "laboratory" under 29 CFR § 1910.1450(b) encompasses facilities where relatively small amounts of hazardous chemicals are used on a nonproduction basis, where chemicals are manipulated on a laboratory scale, where multiple chemical procedures or chemicals are used, where procedures are not part of a production process whose function is to produce commercial quantities of materials, and where protective laboratory practices and equipment are normally used to minimize the potential for employee exposure to hazardous chemicals.

The cornerstone requirement of 29 CFR § 1910.1450(e) mandates that institutions develop and implement a Chemical Hygiene Plan (CHP). This plan must be capable of protecting employees from health hazards associated with hazardous chemicals in the laboratory and must be readily available to employees, their designated representatives, and the Assistant Secretary of Labor for Occupational Safety and Health. The Chemical Hygiene Plan serves as the institution''s primary compliance document and must include standard operating procedures relevant to safety and health considerations when laboratory work involves hazardous chemicals, criteria that the employer will use to determine and implement control measures to reduce employee exposure to hazardous chemicals, and a requirement that fume hoods and other protective equipment are functioning properly.

Under 29 CFR § 1910.1450(e)(3), institutions must designate a Chemical Hygiene Officer who is qualified by training or experience to provide technical guidance in the development and implementation of the Chemical Hygiene Plan. This individual must be capable of providing technical guidance and oversight for laboratory safety programs and ensuring that the Chemical Hygiene Plan remains current and effective. The Chemical Hygiene Officer typically serves as the primary point of contact for laboratory safety matters and coordinates with various departments to ensure institution-wide compliance.

The regulation requires specific provisions within the Chemical Hygiene Plan as outlined in 29 CFR § 1910.1450(e)(3). These provisions must include procedures for use of respirators and other personal protective equipment, circumstances under which a particular laboratory operation, procedure, or activity requires prior approval from the employer or the employer''s designee before implementation, provisions for employee information and training, provisions for medical consultation and medical examinations, designation of personnel responsible for implementation of the Chemical Hygiene Plan, and provisions for additional employee protection for work with particularly hazardous substances.

Training requirements under 29 CFR § 1910.1450(f) mandate that institutions provide employees with information and training to ensure they are apprised of the hazards of chemicals present in their work area. This training must occur at the time of initial assignment to a work area where hazardous chemicals are present and prior to assignments involving new exposure situations. The training must include methods and observations that may be used to detect the presence or release of hazardous chemicals, the physical and health hazards of chemicals in the work area, measures employees can take to protect themselves from these hazards, and applicable details of the employer''s Chemical Hygiene Plan.

Medical surveillance requirements under 29 CFR § 1910.1450(g) establish specific circumstances under which institutions must provide medical attention for laboratory employees. Medical consultation and examination must be provided without cost to the employee, without loss of pay, and at a reasonable time and place when an employee develops signs or symptoms associated with hazardous chemical exposure, when exposure monitoring reveals an exposure level routinely above the action level or permissible exposure limit for an OSHA regulated substance, or whenever an event takes place in the work area such as a spill, leak, explosion, or other occurrence resulting in the likelihood of a hazardous exposure.

The regulation incorporates by reference the permissible exposure limits established in 29 CFR § 1910 Subpart Z for specific regulated substances. When laboratory work involves substances with established permissible exposure limits, institutions must ensure that employee exposures do not exceed these limits. Additionally, 29 CFR § 1910.1450(e)(3)(viii) requires special provisions for work with particularly hazardous substances, including select carcinogens, reproductive toxins, and substances with high acute toxicity.

Recordkeeping obligations under 29 CFR § 1910.1450(j) require institutions to establish and maintain accurate records of any measurements taken to monitor employee exposures and any medical consultation and examination including tests or written opinions required by the standard. Exposure monitoring records must include the date of measurement, operation involving exposure, sampling and analytical methods used, number, duration, and results of samples taken, type of respiratory protective equipment worn, and name and exposure of the employees whose exposures are represented. These records must be kept, transferred, and made available in accordance with 29 CFR § 1910.1020.

Higher education institutions must also comply with the Hazard Communication Standard requirements as they apply to laboratory settings under 29 CFR § 1910.1200. This includes ensuring that Safety Data Sheets are readily accessible to laboratory employees during their work shifts, that chemical containers are properly labeled, and that employees receive information about the hazard communication program as part of their laboratory safety training.

Implementation of respiratory protection programs may be required under certain circumstances as referenced in 29 CFR § 1910.1450(i). When respiratory protection is necessary to maintain exposures below permissible limits, institutions must establish a respiratory protection program in accordance with 29 CFR § 1910.134. This includes medical evaluations, fit testing, training, and maintenance procedures for respiratory protective equipment.

Penalties for non-compliance with the Laboratory Standard can be substantial and are enforced through OSHA''s inspection and citation process. Under the Occupational Safety and Health Act, willful or repeated violations can result in penalties up to $156,259 per violation, while serious violations can result in penalties up to $15,625 per violation. Other-than-serious violations may result in penalties up to $15,625 per violation. Failure to abate violations can result in additional daily penalties. Beyond monetary penalties, institutions may face work stoppage orders, increased regulatory scrutiny, and potential criminal liability in cases involving willful violations that result in worker fatalities.

The regulation does not establish specific reporting deadlines to OSHA for routine compliance activities, but institutions must report work-related fatalities within eight hours and work-related inpatient hospitalizations, amputations, or losses of an eye within twenty-four hours under 29 CFR § 1904.39. Additionally, institutions must maintain required records and make them available to OSHA compliance officers during inspections and to employees or their designated representatives upon request.

For implementation guidance, higher education institutions should begin by conducting a comprehensive assessment of all laboratory operations to determine applicability of the standard. This assessment should identify all locations where hazardous chemicals are used in laboratory settings, catalog the types and quantities of chemicals present, evaluate current safety practices and equipment, and identify personnel who require training under the standard.

Development of the Chemical Hygiene Plan should involve collaboration between the designated Chemical Hygiene Officer, laboratory supervisors, safety professionals, and institutional administration. The plan must be tailored to the specific hazards and operations present at the institution while meeting all regulatory requirements. Regular review and updating of the Chemical Hygiene Plan ensures continued effectiveness and compliance with evolving laboratory operations and regulatory requirements.

Training program development should address the diverse population of laboratory workers in higher education settings, including consideration of varying levels of experience, different academic disciplines, and rotating personnel such as students and visiting researchers. Training documentation should be maintained to demonstrate compliance and identify individuals requiring refresher training or additional specialized training for work with particularly hazardous substances.

Institutions should establish clear procedures for medical surveillance, including identification of healthcare providers qualified to conduct occupational health evaluations, protocols for exposure incidents, and systems for maintaining confidential medical records. Coordination with campus health services or external occupational health providers ensures appropriate medical support for laboratory personnel.

Regular auditing and assessment of laboratory safety programs helps institutions identify compliance gaps and opportunities for improvement. This may include periodic inspections of laboratory facilities, review of incident reports and near-miss events, evaluation of training effectiveness, and assessment of Chemical Hygiene Plan implementation. Documentation of these activities demonstrates institutional commitment to laboratory safety and regulatory compliance.

Institutions should also consider the intersection of the Laboratory Standard with other applicable regulations, including EPA regulations for hazardous waste management, DOT requirements for chemical transportation, and state and local environmental and safety regulations. Coordination between different compliance programs ensures comprehensive management of laboratory chemical hazards while avoiding conflicting requirements or duplicative efforts.

The performance-based nature of the Laboratory Standard provides institutions with flexibility in developing safety programs appropriate to their specific operations while maintaining compliance with regulatory requirements. This approach recognizes the diversity of laboratory operations in higher education settings and allows for innovative safety solutions that effectively protect laboratory personnel from chemical hazards. Successful implementation requires ongoing commitment from institutional leadership, adequate resource allocation, and active participation from laboratory personnel at all levels.

Regular communication with OSHA consultation services, professional organizations, and peer institutions can provide valuable guidance for maintaining effective laboratory safety programs. These resources offer technical assistance, training opportunities, and best practice sharing that supports continuous improvement in laboratory safety performance and regulatory compliance.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:12.244544', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-097', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

Under 33 U.S.C. § 2702, universities are strictly liable for removal costs and damages resulting from oil discharges from their facilities. This liability extends to cleanup costs, natural resource damages, economic losses, and third-party claims. The implementing regulations at 40 CFR Part 112 establish the Spill Prevention, Control, and Countermeasure (SPCC) rule, which applies to facilities with aggregate oil storage capacity exceeding 1,320 gallons above ground or 42,000 gallons underground.

Key compliance requirements include: container inspection protocols under 40 CFR § 112.8, requiring visual inspections of above-ground storage tanks and associated piping systems; integrity testing programs per 40 CFR § 112.9, mandating pressure testing and non-destructive examination of storage vessels; comprehensive recordkeeping systems documenting all inspections, tests, and maintenance activities for minimum five-year periods as specified in 40 CFR § 112.7(e).

Universities must provide annual training to employees handling oil products, covering spill prevention procedures, emergency response protocols, and environmental protection measures under 40 CFR § 112.7(f). Additionally, institutions must conduct annual discharge prevention briefings for all personnel working near oil storage areas, ensuring awareness of pollution prevention measures and reporting procedures.

Enforcement authority rests with the Environmental Protection Agency under 33 U.S.C. § 2716A, which authorizes civil penalties up to $40,000 per day for SPCC violations, with maximum penalties reaching $137,000 per violation under current adjustment rates in 40 CFR Part 19. Criminal penalties under 33 U.S.C. § 2716A(b) include fines up to $250,000 and imprisonment up to five years for knowing violations.

Recent amendments include the 2016 SPCC amendments at 40 CFR Part 112, which modified inspection frequencies for certain storage systems and updated recordkeeping requirements. Universities must maintain current SPCC plans reviewed by Professional Engineers and updated every five years or following significant facility modifications.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:12.273231', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-143', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);


Before ratification of a contract for housing sale or lease, sellers and landlords must:

 	<li style="list-style-type: none">

 	Give an EPA-approved information pamphlet on identifying and controlling lead-based paint hazards ("Protect Your Family From Lead In Your Home" pamphlet).

 	Disclose any known information concerning lead-based paint or lead-based paint hazards. The seller or landlord must also disclose information such as the location of the lead-based paint and/or lead-based paint hazards, and the condition of the painted surfaces.

 	Provide any records and reports on lead-based paint and/or lead-based paint hazards which are available to the seller or landlord (for multi-unit buildings, this requirement includes records and reports concerning common areas and other units, when such information was obtained as a result of a building-wide evaluation).

 	Include an attachment to the contractor lease (or insert language in the lease itself) which includes a Lead Warning Statement and confirms that the seller or landlord has complied with all notification requirements. This attachment is to be provided in the same language used in the rest of the contract. Sellers or landlords, and agents, as well as homebuyers or tenants, must sign and date the attachment.

 	Sellers must provide homebuyers a 10-day period to conduct a paint inspection or risk assessment for lead-based paint or lead-based paint hazards. Parties may mutually agree, in writing, to lengthen or shorten the time period for inspection. Homebuyers may waive this inspection opportunity.

 	Sellers and lessors must retain a copy of the disclosures for no less than three years from the date of sale or the date the leasing period begins.', '', 'Environmental Health & Safety', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:12.301167', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Residential Lead-Based Paint Hazard Reduction Act of 1992, codified at 42 U.S.C. § 4851 et seq., establishes comprehensive federal requirements for disclosure of lead-based paint hazards in residential properties built before 1978. The implementing regulations, found at 24 CFR Part 35 and 40 CFR Part 745, create specific obligations for property owners, including higher education institutions that own or lease residential housing units.

For universities and colleges, this regulation applies when the institution owns more than four residential housing units constructed before 1978, including dormitories, apartment complexes, faculty housing, or graduate student housing. The Act''s disclosure requirements under 42 U.S.C. § 4852d mandate that educational institutions provide comprehensive lead-based paint information to prospective residents before lease execution.

Key statutory provisions require institutions to distribute the EPA-approved pamphlet "Protect Your Family From Lead In Your Home" as specified in 40 CFR § 745.113. Universities must disclose all known information about lead-based paint or hazards, including location, condition of painted surfaces, and any available inspection records or risk assessments as required under 42 U.S.C. § 4852d(a)(1). For multi-unit campus housing, disclosure must include building-wide evaluation results covering common areas and other units per 24 CFR § 35.92.

Compliance documentation requires a Lead Warning Statement attachment to all lease agreements, executed in the same language as the primary contract. Both institutional representatives and student residents must sign and date these disclosures under 42 U.S.C. § 4852d(a)(2). For property sales, universities must provide purchasers a 10-day inspection period, though this provision typically applies to disposition of campus real estate rather than student housing operations.

Enforcement authority rests with EPA under 42 U.S.C. § 4852d(b)(3), with civil penalties up to $37,500 per violation as adjusted under 40 CFR § 19.4. The Department of Housing and Urban Development also maintains concurrent jurisdiction for certain residential properties under 24 CFR § 35.178. Universities must retain disclosure records for three years from lease commencement as mandated by 42 U.S.C. § 4852d(a)(3).

Recent regulatory updates include the 2021 EPA Lead Renovation, Repair, and Painting Rule amendments at 40 CFR § 745.80, which affect campus maintenance operations in pre-1978 housing facilities. The 2019 HUD Lead Safe Housing Rule updates at 24 CFR § 35.86 also impact federally-assisted student housing programs.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:12.301167', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-144', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

 	If the university is a hazardous waste generator it must:

 	Certify there is a program in place to reduce quantity and toxicity of waste to the degree economically practicable;

 	Establish record-keeping practices for waste generated; 3. Use appropriate containers and labeling practices for storage, transport or disposal, and use a manifest system.

 	If the university is a small quantity generator, it must provide to the EPA every year setting out quantities and nature of waste generated, how disposed, efforts to reduce volume and toxicity, and changes in volume and toxicity achieved.

 	Underground Storage Tank training must be conducted every 7 years.', '', 'Environmental Health & Safety', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:12.328611', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Resource Conservation and Recovery Act (RCRA), codified at 42 U.S.C. §§ 6901-6992k, grants the Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) comprehensive authority to regulate hazardous waste from generation to disposal through a comprehensive "cradle-to-grave" management system. Higher education institutions frequently fall under RCRA jurisdiction due to research activities, laboratory operations, maintenance facilities, and campus infrastructure that generate regulated hazardous wastes.

Under 40 CFR Part 262, universities are classified as hazardous waste generators based on monthly generation quantities: Large Quantity Generators (LQGs) produce ≥1,000 kg/month, Small Quantity Generators (SQGs) produce 100-1,000 kg/month, and Very Small Quantity Generators produce <100 kg/month. Each classification carries distinct regulatory obligations.

LQG universities must comply with stringent requirements under 40 CFR §§ 262.11-262.87, including waste minimization certifications on EPA Form 8700-22A, comprehensive recordkeeping for three years per 40 CFR § 262.40, proper containerization and labeling per 40 CFR § 262.34, and hazardous waste manifest usage for off-site shipments. SQG institutions face similar but less intensive requirements under 40 CFR § 262.44, including annual EPA reporting detailing waste quantities, disposal methods, minimization efforts, and toxicity reduction achievements.

RCRA Subtitle I (42 U.S.C. §§ 6991-6991m) governs Underground Storage Tank (UST) systems common at universities for fuel storage. Under 40 CFR Part 280, UST operators must complete EPA-approved training every seven years, maintain leak detection systems, and implement corrosion protection measures. Financial responsibility requirements under 40 CFR § 280.93 mandate universities demonstrate ability to cover cleanup costs up to $1 million per occurrence.

Enforcement mechanisms include administrative orders, civil penalties up to $37,500 per violation per day under 42 U.S.C. § 6928(a), and criminal sanctions for knowing violations. The EPA''s 2020 amendments to 40 CFR Part 262 expanded academic laboratory standards, allowing eligible universities to manage laboratory chemicals under alternative timelines and procedures, reducing compliance burdens while maintaining environmental protection.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:12.328611', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-145', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

 	In addition to bulk email, the law covers all commercial messages, which it defines as "any electronic mail message the primary purpose of which is the commercial advertisement or promotion of a commercial product or service."

 	Each separate email in violation of the CAN-SPAM Act is subject to penalties of up to $16,000.', '', 'Information Technology', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.605229', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Controlling the Assault of Non-Solicited Pornography And Marketing Act of 2003 (CAN-SPAM Act), codified at 15 U.S.C. §§ 7701-7713, establishes comprehensive federal standards for commercial email communications that directly impact higher education institutions'' fundraising and development activities. This regulation governs all commercial electronic mail messages, defined under 15 U.S.C. § 7702(2) as any electronic mail message whose primary purpose is commercial advertisement or promotion of commercial products or services.

For higher education institutions, CAN-SPAM compliance is critical for alumni relations, fundraising campaigns, continuing education promotions, and event marketing. The Act''s provisions under 15 U.S.C. § 7704 mandate several key requirements: (1) prohibition of false or misleading header information, including accurate ''From,'' ''To,'' and routing information; (2) prohibition of deceptive subject lines that mislead recipients about message content; (3) clear identification of commercial messages as advertisements when not sent to existing customers; (4) inclusion of valid physical postal address of sender; (5) provision of clear opt-out mechanisms that remain functional for at least 30 days; and (6) prompt processing of opt-out requests within 10 business days as specified in 15 U.S.C. § 7704(a)(4).

The Federal Trade Commission (FTC) enforces CAN-SPAM violations under 15 U.S.C. § 7706, with civil penalties reaching $46,517 per violation as of 2023 adjustments. Universities must ensure their advancement offices, continuing education divisions, and third-party vendors comply with these standards. The Act''s extraterritorial reach under 15 U.S.C. § 7703 covers emails sent to U.S. recipients regardless of sender location, affecting international recruitment communications. Recent FTC enforcement actions have emphasized the importance of vendor oversight, making institutional compliance programs essential for managing email marketing relationships with external fundraising consultants and technology providers.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.605229', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-103', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

Higher education institutions typically fall under the "used oil generator" category defined in 40 CFR § 279.20, which encompasses entities producing used oil through vehicle maintenance, laboratory operations, or facility management. Universities must comply with strict storage requirements under 40 CFR § 279.22, including using containers and tanks in good condition, properly labeling all storage units with "Used Oil" markings, and preventing releases to the environment. Storage containers must be closed except when adding or removing used oil, and institutions must maintain secondary containment systems for tanks exceeding 1,320 gallons.

Recordkeeping obligations under 40 CFR § 279.27 require universities to maintain detailed logs of used oil quantities generated, stored, and shipped off-site. Records must be retained for three years and include dates of generation, storage locations, and disposal methods. Transportation requirements mandate that institutions can only transfer used oil to facilities with valid EPA identification numbers, as specified in 40 CFR § 279.24.

Enforcement mechanisms include EPA inspections, administrative orders, and civil penalties up to $70,117 per violation per day under 42 U.S.C. § 6928. The 2020 amendments to 40 CFR § 279.10 expanded the definition of used oil to include synthetic oils and updated labeling requirements. Universities must also comply with state-specific requirements, as many states have adopted more stringent standards than federal minimums. Non-compliance can result in criminal penalties under 42 U.S.C. § 6928(d) for knowing violations, including fines up to $50,000 per day and imprisonment. Institutions must designate qualified personnel for used oil management and ensure proper training on handling procedures, emergency response, and regulatory compliance requirements.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:12.359853', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-146', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

 	Requires prompt notification of the release of a select agent outside of the biocontainment area, or of theft or loss of a select agent.

 	The university must also deny access to the agents/toxins by restricted persons.', '', 'Environmental Health & Safety', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:12.387646', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Public Health Security and Bioterrorism Preparedness and Response Act of 2002, codified at 18 U.S.C. § 175b and implemented through 42 CFR Part 73, establishes comprehensive federal oversight of select agents and toxins that pose severe threats to public health and safety. This regulation applies to all higher education institutions conducting research with biological agents and toxins listed in 42 CFR § 73.3 and § 73.4, including dangerous pathogens such as Bacillus anthracis, Francisella tularensis, and Ebola virus.

Under 42 CFR § 73.6, universities must register with the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) and obtain approval before possessing, using, or transferring select agents. The registration process requires submission of detailed facility information, security plans, and personnel screening protocols through the CDC''s Select Agent Program. Universities must designate a Responsible Official under 42 CFR § 73.10 who serves as the primary point of contact and ensures institutional compliance.

Personnel access restrictions under 18 U.S.C. § 175b(d)(1) prohibit institutions from granting access to individuals who are restricted persons, including those with criminal histories involving controlled substances, mental health adjudications, or non-immigrant visa status restrictions. All personnel must undergo FBI background checks and receive approval before accessing select agents.

Immediate reporting obligations under 42 CFR § 73.19 require universities to notify the CDC within 24 hours of theft, loss, release, or exposure incidents involving select agents. Annual reports detailing inventory changes, research activities, and security measures must be submitted by March 31st each year per 42 CFR § 73.20.

Violations carry severe penalties under 18 U.S.C. § 175b, including criminal prosecution with fines up to $250,000 and imprisonment up to five years for individuals, and civil penalties up to $500,000 per violation for institutions. The 2019 amendments strengthened cybersecurity requirements and expanded reporting obligations for incidents involving information systems containing select agent data.

Universities must maintain comprehensive security plans addressing physical security, personnel reliability, information security, and incident response protocols. Regular inspections by CDC officials ensure ongoing compliance with biosafety and security standards outlined in 42 CFR Part 73.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:12.387646', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-147', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

 	The Act requires the identification, inventory, marking and quarterly inspection of PCB transformers.', '', 'Environmental Health & Safety', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:12.415976', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Toxic Substances Control Act (TSCA) of 1976, codified at 15 U.S.C. §§ 2601-2629, grants the Environmental Protection Agency comprehensive authority to regulate chemical substances that pose unreasonable risks to human health and the environment. Higher education institutions must comply with TSCA''s stringent requirements, particularly regarding polychlorinated biphenyls (PCBs) commonly found in electrical equipment and transformers on university campuses.

Under 15 U.S.C. § 2605 and implementing regulations at 40 CFR Part 761, universities must identify, inventory, and properly manage PCB-containing equipment. The Act prohibits the manufacture, processing, distribution, and use of PCBs except in totally enclosed systems, with specific exceptions outlined in 40 CFR § 761.30. Educational institutions operating electrical transformers containing 50 ppm or greater PCB concentrations must register these units with EPA and comply with strict operational standards.

Key compliance obligations include quarterly visual inspections documented per 40 CFR § 761.30(a)(1)(ix), proper marking with standardized PCB labels as specified in 40 CFR § 761.40, and maintenance of detailed inventory records. Universities must conduct annual electrical tests for PCB transformers and maintain inspection logs for EPA review. Storage of PCB waste must comply with 40 CFR § 761.65, requiring secure facilities meeting specific construction standards and regular monitoring protocols.

Disposal requirements under 40 CFR Part 761 Subpart D mandate that PCB transformers be decontaminated or disposed of through EPA-approved methods within one year of removal from service. Universities must use authorized treatment and disposal facilities listed in EPA databases and maintain chain-of-custody documentation.

Violations carry severe penalties under 15 U.S.C. § 2615, including civil penalties up to $37,500 per violation per day, with criminal sanctions for knowing or willful violations reaching $25,000 daily and potential imprisonment. Recent amendments through the Frank R. Lautenberg Chemical Safety for the 21st Century Act of 2016 strengthened EPA''s enforcement authority and expanded reporting requirements for educational institutions managing chemical inventories exceeding threshold quantities.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:12.415976', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-148', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

The regulation applies broadly to higher education personnel involved in federal programs, including administrators overseeing federal grants under 2 CFR Part 200, financial aid officers managing Title IV programs under 20 U.S.C. § 1070 et seq., and research faculty participating in federal research contracts. Coverage extends to spouses, minor children, general partners, organizations where the employee serves as officer or trustee, and any person with whom contractual arrangements exist that could be affected by government actions.

Key provisions under 18 U.S.C. § 208(a) create criminal liability for knowing participation in conflicted matters, with penalties including fines up to $50,000 and imprisonment up to five years. The Office of Government Ethics (OGE) provides regulatory guidance through 5 CFR Part 2635, establishing standards for identifying covered relationships and financial interests. Violations may also result in administrative sanctions including suspension, removal, or debarment from federal programs under 2 CFR § 180.800.

Higher education institutions must implement robust compliance frameworks addressing common conflict scenarios: faculty serving on federal peer review panels while holding grants from the same agencies; administrators making procurement decisions involving vendors in which they hold stock; and university officials participating in federal funding determinations affecting institutional partnerships. The regulation requires prospective analysis of potential conflicts before participation in any federal matter.

Exceptions exist under 18 U.S.C. § 208(b)(1)-(4), including de minimis financial interests below statutory thresholds updated annually by OGE, diversified mutual funds meeting 5 CFR § 2635.403(b) requirements, and matters of general applicability affecting broad classes of similarly situated persons. Written advance authorization may be obtained when conflicts are not substantial or participation serves government interests.

Recent amendments through the STOCK Act of 2012 enhanced disclosure requirements, while Executive Order 13989 (2021) strengthened ethics enforcement mechanisms. Institutions must establish ongoing monitoring systems, mandatory disclosure protocols, and remediation procedures including divestiture, recusal, or assignment of duties to avoid violations that could jeopardize federal funding eligibility totaling billions annually across higher education.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:12.444286', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-164', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

**Legal Authority and Key Provisions**

18 U.S.C. § 201(b) criminalizes the offer, promise, or giving of anything of value to any public official with intent to influence official acts or induce violations of lawful duties. Subsection (c) prohibits giving gratuities to public officials for acts already performed. The statute defines "public official" broadly under 18 U.S.C. § 201(a)(1) to include federal employees, military personnel, and individuals acting on behalf of the United States government.

For higher education institutions, this regulation has particular significance given extensive interactions with federal agencies including the Department of Education, National Science Foundation, National Institutes of Health, and other grant-making bodies. University officials, faculty, and staff must avoid any conduct that could constitute improper influence of federal decision-makers.

**Scope and Higher Education Applications**

The regulation applies when universities engage federal officials regarding:
- Federal research grants and contracts under 2 CFR Part 200 (Uniform Guidance)
- Student financial aid administration under Title IV of the Higher Education Act (20 U.S.C. § 1070 et seq.)
- Regulatory compliance matters with Department of Education officials
- Congressional lobbying activities subject to the Lobbying Disclosure Act (2 U.S.C. § 1601)
- Federal procurement opportunities exceeding $25,000 under 41 U.S.C. § 4712

**Enforcement and Penalties**

18 U.S.C. § 201(b)(1) and (b)(2) establish penalties up to 15 years imprisonment and fines up to three times the monetary equivalent of the bribe for bribery offenses. Gratuity violations under § 201(c) carry penalties up to two years imprisonment. The Department of Justice prosecutes violations, often resulting in debarment from federal contracting under the Federal Acquisition Regulation 48 CFR 9.406.

Institutions may face suspension or termination of federal funding under 2 CFR 180.800 for employee misconduct. The Office of Inspector General within relevant agencies investigates suspected violations and coordinates with federal prosecutors.

**Recent Developments**

The Supreme Court''s decision in McDonnell v. United States (2016) refined the definition of "official act" under § 201, requiring more specific quid pro quo arrangements for bribery convictions. However, universities must maintain conservative compliance approaches as lower courts continue interpreting these standards. Executive Order 13770 (2017) imposed additional ethics restrictions on federal employee interactions that indirectly affect university lobbying practices.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:12.473694', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-165', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

**Legal Authority and Scope**
Section 203 operates in conjunction with 18 U.S.C. § 205 (activities of officers and employees in claims against and other matters affecting the Government) and is enforced by the Department of Justice. The statute prohibits covered individuals from receiving compensation for representational services before any department, agency, court, or court-martial regarding matters in which the United States is a party or has a direct and substantial interest.

**Key Provisions and Higher Education Applications**
For universities, this regulation creates critical compliance challenges when engaging current or former federal officials as consultants, board members, or representatives. Under 18 U.S.C. § 203(a), current federal employees cannot receive compensation for representing third parties in federal matters. The post-employment restrictions under § 203(c) prohibit former senior federal officials from representing clients before their former agencies for two years after leaving federal service.

Higher education institutions must exercise particular caution when retaining former Department of Education officials, NIH administrators, or NSF personnel for grant applications, regulatory compliance matters, or advocacy activities. The "direct and substantial interest" standard encompasses virtually all federal funding decisions, regulatory proceedings, and policy determinations affecting universities.

**Enforcement and Penalties**
Violations constitute felony offenses punishable by fines up to $50,000 and imprisonment up to two years under 18 U.S.C. § 203(f). The Office of Government Ethics provides interpretive guidance through 5 CFR Part 2635, while individual agencies maintain supplemental ethics regulations. Universities face potential criminal liability, civil penalties, and debarment from federal programs under the Federal Acquisition Regulation (48 CFR Part 9.4).

**Compliance Requirements**
Institutions must implement robust due diligence procedures for all consultant agreements, board appointments, and representational arrangements. This includes verification of federal employment history, assessment of potential conflicts under 18 U.S.C. § 207 (post-employment restrictions), and ongoing monitoring of activities. Universities should maintain comprehensive records of all engagements and establish clear protocols for identifying prohibited representational activities before federal agencies.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:12.501342', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-169', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

 	A private university that does not employ a lobbyist is subject to restrictions.', '', 'Ethics & Governance', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:12.5297', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Ethics in Government Act, as codified in 5 U.S.C. § 7353 and subsequently amended by the Ethics Reform Act and the Honest Leadership and Open Government Act of 2007, establishes comprehensive ethical standards governing federal employees'' acceptance of gifts and benefits. This regulation creates a strict framework prohibiting members of Congress, executive branch officials, and federal employees from soliciting or accepting anything of value from entities seeking official action, conducting regulated business, or whose interests may be substantially affected by official duties.

For higher education institutions, compliance obligations arise when universities engage with federal agencies, receive federal funding, or interact with government officials in official capacities. Under 5 U.S.C. § 7353(a), federal employees are prohibited from accepting gifts from ''prohibited sources'' - defined as entities seeking official action from the employee''s agency or whose interests may be substantially affected by the employee''s duties. Universities fall within this definition when seeking grants, contracts, regulatory approvals, or other federal benefits.

Key provisions include absolute prohibitions on gifts exceeding $20 per occasion or $50 aggregate annually from the same source, as specified in 5 CFR § 2635.204. The regulation encompasses tangible items, services, entertainment, hospitality, loans, and any benefit with monetary value. Exceptions exist for widely distributed promotional items under $20, items given because of personal relationships unrelated to official duties, and certain educational benefits under 5 CFR § 2635.204(h).

Enforcement mechanisms include disciplinary action ranging from reprimands to termination for federal employees, while universities may face contract suspension, debarment, or funding restrictions. The Office of Government Ethics oversees compliance through 5 CFR Part 2635, requiring agencies to establish supplemental standards. Universities must implement robust compliance programs including gift policies, training protocols, and monitoring systems to prevent inadvertent violations when interacting with federal personnel.

Recent amendments under the Honest Leadership and Open Government Act expanded disclosure requirements and strengthened enforcement mechanisms. Universities receiving federal research funding exceeding $1 million annually must establish conflict of interest policies addressing federal employee interactions, as mandated by 2 CFR § 200.112. Non-compliance can result in civil monetary penalties up to $50,000 per violation under 31 U.S.C. § 3802, making proactive compliance essential for institutional risk management.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:12.5297', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-173', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

Under 2 U.S.C. § 4725 and implementing regulations, both chambers prohibit acceptance of gifts from registered lobbyists or any private entity that retains or employs registered lobbyists. For higher education institutions, this creates significant compliance obligations when universities employ registered lobbyists or retain lobbying firms under the Lobbying Disclosure Act (2 U.S.C. § 1601 et seq.).

The $50 gift threshold under House Rule 25(5)(a)(1)(A) and Senate Rule 35.1(a)(1) applies to non-lobbying entities, including most public and private universities not engaged in lobbying activities. Permissible gifts include meals at educational briefings, conference materials, and nominal promotional items, provided each item''s value remains below $50 per recipient and the aggregate annual value from any single source does not exceed $100 per recipient.

Higher education institutions must distinguish between educational activities and lobbying when hosting congressional members. Under 2 U.S.C. § 1602(7), educational briefings on policy matters may qualify for exceptions if they provide informational value rather than advocacy. However, institutions registered under the Lobbying Disclosure Act cannot provide any gifts, regardless of educational purpose.

Enforcement mechanisms include referral to the House Committee on Ethics (House Rule 25(7)) and Senate Select Committee on Ethics (Senate Rule 35.7). Violations may result in formal sanctions, public disclosure, and potential criminal referral under 18 U.S.C. § 201 for serious breaches. The Ethics in Government Act of 1978, as amended by the Stop Trading on Congressional Knowledge Act (Pub. L. 112-105), requires annual financial disclosure of gifts exceeding statutory thresholds.

Recent amendments effective January 2023 clarified that virtual event access and digital materials are subject to the same valuation standards as physical items. Institutions must maintain detailed records of all interactions with congressional offices, including documentation of gift values and recipient confirmation of compliance with applicable rules.

Universities engaging with Congress must implement robust compliance programs, including staff training on gift restrictions, approval processes for congressional interactions, and coordination with registered lobbying activities to ensure full regulatory compliance.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:12.55872', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-181', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

Under 5 U.S.C. app. 4 § 501(a), covered noncareer employees are prohibited from receiving any outside earned income exceeding 15% of the annual rate of basic pay for level II of the Executive Schedule, currently approximately $32,000 annually. This limitation applies to compensation from any source outside the employee''s government position, including consulting fees, speaking honoraria, board service compensation, and teaching income from private institutions.

Section 502 defines "outside earned income" broadly to encompass wages, salaries, fees, and other compensation for personal services, excluding investment income, pensions, and certain grandfathered arrangements. For university presidents and senior administrators at institutions like military academies or federally-controlled universities, this creates significant restrictions on traditional academic income sources such as book royalties, conference speaking fees, and private sector consulting.

The regulation''s scope, detailed in 5 U.S.C. app. 4 § 505, applies to Presidential appointees subject to Senate confirmation, noncareer Senior Executive Service members, and Schedule C appointees compensated at GS-15 level or above. In higher education contexts, this typically includes university presidents, provosts, and other senior administrators at federal service academies, government research institutions, and federally-chartered universities.

Compliance monitoring occurs through annual financial disclosure requirements under 5 U.S.C. app. 4 § 101, with institutions'' designated agency ethics officials (DAEOs) responsible for oversight. Violations may result in disciplinary action including removal from position, civil penalties up to $50,000 under 5 U.S.C. app. 4 § 104, and potential criminal prosecution for willful violations.

Recent Office of Government Ethics guidance clarified that virtual speaking engagements and online course instruction constitute covered activities subject to income limitations. The 2023 amendments strengthened enforcement mechanisms and expanded reporting requirements for cryptocurrency and digital asset compensation. Universities must establish robust tracking systems to monitor covered employees'' outside activities and ensure compliance with both income thresholds and prior approval requirements for any outside employment or business relationships.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:12.653814', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-064', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

18 U.S.C. § 207(a) imposes a permanent bar on former federal employees from representing third parties in communications or appearances before any federal agency regarding particular matters in which they personally and substantially participated while in government service. The statute''s lifetime prohibition under § 207(a)(1) applies to any matter that directly and predictably affects the financial interests of parties involved. Section 207(c) extends restrictions to senior employees, including those at GS-17 level and above, political appointees, and Senior Executive Service members, prohibiting them from representing others before their former agencies for two years post-employment.

For higher education institutions, compliance is critical when engaging former federal officials in roles involving government relations, grant applications, or regulatory advocacy. Universities must implement screening procedures under § 207(j) to identify potential conflicts before hiring former federal personnel. The Office of Government Ethics (OGE) provides interpretive guidance through 5 CFR Part 2637, clarifying that institutions may face criminal liability if they knowingly permit violations.

Violations carry severe penalties under 18 U.S.C. § 207(i), including criminal fines up to $250,000 and imprisonment up to five years for willful violations. The Department of Justice prosecutes criminal violations, while OGE handles administrative enforcement. Universities must establish compliance programs including conflict screening protocols, employee training on restriction recognition, and documentation systems for due diligence efforts.

Recent amendments through the Stop Trading on Congressional Knowledge Act strengthened disclosure requirements for senior officials. The regulation''s scope encompasses all communications with federal agencies, including informal contacts and written submissions, making comprehensive compliance programs essential for higher education institutions engaging in federal advocacy or seeking government funding.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:12.682788', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-194', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

 	For charitable contributions of $250 or more, the donor must receive a contemporaneous written acknowledgment from the organization of the gift.

 	The acknowledgment should note the amount of any cash contribution and, if the donation is in the form of property, the acknowledgment must describe, but need not value the property.  Valuation of the property is the responsibility of the donor.', '', 'Fundraising & Development', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.703034', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Internal Revenue Code''s substantiation and disclosure provisions, codified at 26 U.S.C. § 170, establish mandatory requirements for charitable contribution acknowledgments by tax-exempt organizations, including higher education institutions. These provisions, effective for contributions made after December 31, 1993, are further detailed in Treasury Regulations 26 CFR § 1.170A-13.

For contributions of $250 or more, universities must provide donors with contemporaneous written acknowledgments containing specific elements as prescribed under 26 U.S.C. § 170(f)(8). The acknowledgment must include: (1) the amount of any cash contribution; (2) a description (but not valuation) of any non-cash property contributed; (3) a statement of whether the organization provided any goods or services in consideration for the contribution; and (4) if goods or services were provided, a description and good faith estimate of their value.

Contemporaneous acknowledgment, as defined in 26 CFR § 1.170A-13(f)(3), means the donor receives the acknowledgment by the earlier of: the date the donor files the tax return for the year of contribution, or the due date (including extensions) of that return. Universities may provide acknowledgments via written letter, email, or other written communication that contains all required elements.

For contributions exceeding $75 where the university provides goods or services, institutions must furnish written disclosure statements under 26 U.S.C. § 170(f)(8)(B). These disclosures must inform donors that their deductible contribution amount equals the excess of their payment over the value of goods or services received.

Substantiation requirements under 26 CFR § 1.170A-13(b) vary by contribution amount: donations under $250 require canceled checks or receipts; contributions of $250-$500 need written acknowledgments; donations exceeding $500 require additional documentation including acquisition information; and non-cash contributions over $5,000 mandate qualified appraisals per 26 U.S.C. § 170(f)(11).

Universities face significant penalties for non-compliance. Under 26 U.S.C. § 6714, institutions may incur penalties of $10 per contribution (maximum $5,000 per event) for failing to provide required disclosure statements. Additionally, donors may lose deduction eligibility for contributions lacking proper substantiation, potentially damaging university-donor relationships.

Recent amendments through the Pension Protection Act of 2006 eliminated the deduction for contributions of clothing and household items unless in good or better condition, and raised substantiation thresholds. The Tax Cuts and Jobs Act of 2017 maintained these requirements while modifying other charitable deduction provisions. Universities must maintain current knowledge of evolving Treasury guidance and IRS publications, particularly Publication 1771, which provides detailed compliance instructions for tax-exempt organizations regarding charitable contribution substantiation and disclosure obligations.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.703034', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-247', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

Under 18 U.S.C. § 209(a), any officer or employee of the United States who receives compensation from any source other than the United States for services as such officer or employee commits a federal offense punishable by fine and imprisonment up to one year. The Office of Government Ethics (OGE) provides interpretive guidance through 5 CFR Part 2635, which clarifies that this prohibition extends to employees of federally-funded research institutions and universities.

For higher education institutions, this regulation significantly impacts faculty and staff involved in federally-funded research programs, ROTC units, and cooperative education agreements. University employees holding concurrent federal positions, such as researchers with federal lab appointments or military personnel in academic roles, must ensure their compensation structure complies with these restrictions. The prohibition covers direct salary payments, consulting fees, honoraria, and any other form of monetary compensation for official duties.

Key provisions under 5 CFR § 2635.204 specify that the prohibition applies regardless of whether the outside source is a private entity, state government, or foreign organization. Universities must implement robust oversight mechanisms to identify potential violations, particularly in research collaborations where faculty may receive funding from multiple sources.

Enforcement mechanisms include criminal prosecution under 18 U.S.C. § 209, administrative sanctions through employing agencies, and civil penalties. Violations can result in fines up to $50,000, imprisonment, and immediate termination of federal employment. The Department of Justice has prosecuted numerous cases involving dual compensation schemes.

Recent interpretations by OGE have clarified that the statute applies to indirect compensation arrangements, including situations where third parties pay universities specifically to cover salaries of federal employees. Universities must maintain clear documentation separating federal employee compensation from other funding sources to ensure compliance with these stringent requirements.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:12.711005', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-195', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

1. Register with DDTC if regularly engaged in manufacturing or exporting defense articles
2. Obtain export licenses before sharing USML technical data with foreign persons
3. Implement technical data release protocols and access controls
4. Maintain current awareness of USML modifications and regulatory updates
5. Establish robust compliance programs covering all defense-related research activities', 'Research', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:12.738962', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Arms Export Control Act (AECA) of 1976, codified at 22 U.S.C. §§ 2751-2799aa-2, establishes comprehensive federal authority over the export of defense articles, services, and technical data. For higher education institutions, the AECA operates through the International Traffic in Arms Regulations (ITAR), implemented at 22 CFR Parts 120-130, which directly impacts university research, international collaborations, and foreign national participation in defense-related activities.

Under 22 U.S.C. § 2778, the Secretary of State maintains the United States Munitions List (USML), defining defense articles subject to export controls. Universities conducting research involving USML items must obtain appropriate licenses before sharing technical data with foreign persons, including international students, visiting researchers, or collaborative partners. The definition of "export" under 22 CFR § 120.17 includes not only physical shipment but also oral, written, electronic, or visual disclosure of technical data to foreign nationals within the United States.

Higher education institutions face significant compliance obligations under 22 CFR § 120.11, which defines "defense services" to include training, technical assistance, and consulting services related to defense articles. Universities must implement robust compliance programs addressing: registration requirements under 22 CFR Part 122 for institutions regularly engaged in manufacturing or exporting defense articles; licensing procedures under 22 CFR Part 123 for temporary and permanent exports; and technical data release protocols under 22 CFR Part 125.

Enforcement authority rests with the Directorate of Defense Trade Controls (DDTC) under 22 CFR Part 128. Violations may result in civil penalties up to $1,049,820 per violation under 22 U.S.C. § 2778(e), criminal penalties including fines up to $1 million and 20 years imprisonment under 22 U.S.C. § 2778(c), and debarment from export privileges. The State Department''s consent agreements with universities, including recent settlements exceeding $750,000, demonstrate active enforcement in academic settings.

Recent regulatory developments include the 2020-2021 ITAR reforms transferring certain items to Commerce Department jurisdiction under the Export Administration Regulations, and enhanced cybersecurity requirements for technical data storage and transmission under 22 CFR § 120.50. Universities must maintain current awareness of USML modifications and categorical exemption updates affecting fundamental research under 22 CFR § 120.11(8).', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:12.738962', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-034', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

The Act empowers the Bureau of Industry and Security (BIS) within the Department of Commerce to administer the Export Administration Regulations (EAR) at 15 CFR Parts 730-774. These regulations control exports of items listed on the Commerce Control List (15 CFR Part 774) and apply to all U.S. persons, including higher education institutions, regardless of location.

For universities, compliance is critical given extensive international research collaborations, foreign student populations, and technology transfer activities. The regulations cover physical exports, deemed exports (release of controlled technology to foreign nationals within the U.S.), and re-exports of U.S.-origin items. Key provisions include licensing requirements for controlled items (15 CFR Part 748), export control classification requests (15 CFR § 748.3), and recordkeeping obligations (15 CFR Part 762).

Higher education institutions must establish export control compliance programs addressing research security, visiting scholars, international partnerships, and technology licensing. The fundamental research exclusion (15 CFR § 734.8) provides limited relief for basic research ordinarily published, but applied research and restricted access projects typically require licenses.

Violations carry severe penalties under 50 U.S.C. § 4819, including criminal fines up to $1 million per violation, imprisonment up to 20 years, and civil penalties up to $300,000 or twice the transaction value. Administrative sanctions may include denial of export privileges, effectively barring institutions from international activities.

Recent amendments under the Export Control Reform Act strengthen controls on emerging technologies, expand extraterritorial jurisdiction, and enhance enforcement capabilities. Universities must monitor the Entity List (15 CFR Part 744, Supplement 4) restricting transactions with designated foreign parties and comply with new foreign direct product rules affecting research collaborations.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:12.767898', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-037', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

1. Screen all parties against OFAC''s Specially Designated Nationals (SDN) List and consolidated sanctions list before any transactions
2. Block assets of designated persons and file Blocked Property Reports within 10 business days
3. Obtain proper OFAC licensing before engaging with sanctioned entities or jurisdictions
4. Implement robust compliance programs with regular screening procedures and staff training
5. Maintain documentation and seek legal consultation for complex international arrangements', 'Research', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:12.796938', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Foreign Assets Control Regulations (FACR), codified at 31 CFR Parts 500-599, represent a comprehensive framework of economic sanctions administered by the Treasury Department''s Office of Foreign Assets Control (OFAC). These regulations implement various statutory authorities including the International Emergency Economic Powers Act (50 U.S.C. § 1701-1706), the Trading with the Enemy Act (50 U.S.C. § 4301 et seq.), and the Cuba Assets Control Regulations (31 CFR Part 515). For higher education institutions, FACR compliance presents complex challenges involving international research collaborations, student exchanges, and educational programs.

The regulations prohibit U.S. persons from engaging in transactions with designated foreign nationals, entities, and countries without proper OFAC licensing. Higher education institutions must navigate these restrictions when conducting research partnerships, accepting donations, enrolling international students, or operating study abroad programs in sanctioned jurisdictions. The scope extends to any transaction involving U.S. jurisdiction, including dollar-denominated payments processed through U.S. financial institutions.

Key provisions under 31 CFR § 501.601 require institutions to block assets of designated persons and file Blocked Property Reports within 10 business days. Universities must screen all parties against OFAC''s Specially Designated Nationals (SDN) List and consolidated sanctions list before engaging in transactions. Academic activities may qualify for general licenses under 31 CFR § 515.564 for educational exchanges with Cuba, or specific licenses for research collaborations with sanctioned entities.

Enforcement mechanisms include civil monetary penalties up to $307,922 per violation for non-egregious cases and up to $1,503,470 for egregious violations under 31 CFR § 501.805. Criminal penalties may reach $1,000,000 and 20 years imprisonment per 50 U.S.C. § 1705(c). Recent enforcement actions against universities include NYU''s $122,000 settlement for Cuba-related violations and potential violations involving Iranian students'' stipend payments.

Recent amendments include expanded sectoral sanctions on Russia under Executive Order 13662 and enhanced due diligence requirements for financial institutions processing educational transactions. Universities must implement robust compliance programs including regular screening procedures, staff training, and legal consultation for complex international arrangements involving potentially sanctioned parties or jurisdictions.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:12.796938', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-039', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

 	In the event of an actual attack on the United States, the President can also confiscate property connected with a country, group, or person that aided in the attack.', '', 'Research', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:12.826176', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The International Emergency Economic Powers Act (IEEPA), codified at 50 U.S.C. §§ 1701-1707, grants the President extraordinary authority to regulate international commerce during national emergencies. This Act significantly impacts higher education institutions engaged in international activities, research collaborations, and financial transactions.

**Legal Authority and Scope**
Under 50 U.S.C. § 1701, the President may declare national emergencies when facing "unusual and extraordinary threats" to U.S. national security, foreign policy, or economy originating substantially outside the United States. Once declared, 50 U.S.C. § 1702 authorizes comprehensive economic sanctions including asset freezes, transaction prohibitions, and property confiscation.

**Higher Education Applications**
Universities must comply with IEEPA through Treasury''s Office of Foreign Assets Control (OFAC) regulations at 31 CFR Chapter V. Institutions face restrictions on financial transactions with sanctioned entities, research collaborations with designated foreign persons, and technology transfers to prohibited countries. The Act applies to tuition payments from sanctioned regions, international partnerships, and scholarly exchanges.

**Key Compliance Requirements**
Institutions must screen all international transactions against OFAC''s Specially Designated Nationals (SDN) List pursuant to 31 CFR § 501.603. Universities cannot provide educational services to sanctioned individuals without specific licenses under 31 CFR § 501.801. Research agreements with foreign entities require careful vetting to avoid prohibited technology transfers under 31 CFR § 560.204.

**Enforcement and Penalties**
Violations carry severe consequences under 50 U.S.C. § 1705, including criminal penalties up to $1 million and 20 years imprisonment, plus civil penalties reaching $250,000 per violation. OFAC actively investigates higher education compliance, as demonstrated in recent enforcement actions against universities for unlicensed transactions with Iranian and Cuban entities.

**Recent Developments**
Executive orders have expanded IEEPA applications to cybersecurity threats, foreign election interference, and technology sector investments. The 2021 Executive Order 14032 specifically targets educational technology transfers, requiring enhanced due diligence for international research partnerships involving critical technologies.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:12.826176', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-059', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

 	The university must provide a written disclosure statement to the donor(s) who make payments described as quid pro quo contributions in excess of $75.', '', 'Fundraising & Development', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.730525', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Pension Protection Act of 2006, codified in 26 U.S.C. § 170(f)(8) and (f)(17), establishes critical substantiation and disclosure requirements for charitable contributions to higher education institutions. This federal regulation significantly impacts university fundraising operations by mandating specific documentation standards for tax-deductible donations.

Under 26 U.S.C. § 170(f)(8), no charitable deduction is permitted for contributions of $250 or more unless the donor obtains contemporaneous written acknowledgment from the recipient organization. The acknowledgment must contain: (1) the amount of cash contributed and description of non-cash property; (2) a statement of whether goods or services were provided in exchange for the contribution; and (3) if goods or services were provided, a description and good faith estimate of their value. The acknowledgment is considered contemporaneous if obtained by the earlier of the date the return is filed or the due date for filing.

The quid pro quo contribution disclosure requirement, established under 26 U.S.C. § 170(f)(8)(A) and Treasury Regulation 1.170A-13(f)(2), applies when universities receive payments exceeding $75 that are partly charitable contributions and partly payments for goods or services. Universities must provide written disclosure statements informing donors that only the portion exceeding the fair market value of benefits received is tax-deductible, along with a good faith estimate of those benefits'' value.

Higher education institutions face specific compliance challenges given their diverse fundraising activities, including athletic events, galas, alumni functions, and premium gift programs. The IRS has clarified through Revenue Ruling 86-63 that benefits such as preferred seating, parking privileges, or merchandise require valuation and disclosure when the total payment exceeds thresholds.

Enforcement mechanisms include IRS audits of both institutions and donors, potential penalties under 26 U.S.C. § 6721 for failure to provide required statements (up to $310 per statement in 2023), and disallowance of donor deductions. The Tax Cuts and Jobs Act of 2017 amended these provisions, particularly affecting athletic seating rights under 26 U.S.C. § 170(l).

Universities must maintain comprehensive documentation systems, train development staff on compliance requirements, and implement quality assurance procedures to ensure all acknowledgments meet federal standards. Non-compliance can result in donor relations issues, IRS scrutiny, and potential loss of tax-exempt status under extreme circumstances.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.730525', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-248', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

1. Obtain Technical Assistance Agreements or Manufacturing License Agreements before engaging in controlled research collaborations
2. Register with the Directorate of Defense Trade Controls if regularly engaged in ITAR-controlled activities
3. Screen foreign nationals for access to controlled technology and conduct pre-publication reviews of research materials
4. Implement written policies addressing deemed exports when foreign persons access controlled technical data
5. Maintain detailed records of all ITAR-controlled activities and transactions', 'Research', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:12.853271', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The International Traffic in Arms Regulations (ITAR), codified at 22 CFR Parts 120-130 and authorized by the Arms Export Control Act at 22 U.S.C. § 2778, constitute a comprehensive regulatory framework governing the export and temporary import of defense articles, defense services, and related technical data. Administered by the U.S. Department of State''s Directorate of Defense Trade Controls (DDTC), ITAR significantly impacts higher education institutions engaged in research, international collaboration, and technology transfer activities.

Under 22 CFR § 120.6, ITAR applies to any export of defense articles or services listed on the United States Munitions List (USML), found at 22 CFR § 121.1. For universities, this includes controlled research data, technical assistance to foreign persons, and educational exchanges involving defense-related technologies. The regulation defines "export" broadly under 22 CFR § 120.17 to include oral, written, electronic, or visual disclosure of technical data to foreign persons, whether in the United States or abroad.

Higher education institutions must obtain appropriate licenses from DDTC before engaging in ITAR-controlled activities. Technical Assistance Agreements (TAA) under 22 CFR § 124.1 are required for ongoing research collaborations, while Manufacturing License Agreements (MLA) under 22 CFR § 124.2 govern technology transfer arrangements. Universities must also comply with registration requirements under 22 CFR § 122.1 if regularly engaged in ITAR-controlled activities.

Key compliance obligations include implementing robust export control programs, conducting pre-publication reviews of research materials, screening foreign nationals for access to controlled technology, and maintaining detailed records per 22 CFR § 122.5. Universities must establish written policies addressing deemed exports, which occur when foreign persons gain access to controlled technical data within the United States under 22 CFR § 120.17(a)(4).

Violations carry severe penalties under 22 U.S.C. § 2778(c), including criminal fines up to $1 million per violation, imprisonment up to 20 years, and civil penalties reaching $500,000 per violation under 22 CFR § 127.10. Administrative sanctions may include debarment from export privileges and consent agreements requiring enhanced compliance measures.

Recent amendments through the Export Control Reform Act of 2018 have refined USML categories and enhanced interagency coordination. Universities must stay current with Federal Register updates and DDTC guidance documents to maintain compliance with evolving requirements.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:12.853271', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-060', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

1. Screen all international students, researchers, and partners against OFAC Specially Designated Nationals (SDN) List
2. Block assets under university control belonging to sanctioned entities
3. Obtain proper licenses before conducting transactions with sanctioned countries
4. Implement comprehensive sanctions compliance programs with annual risk assessments
5. Maintain detailed recordkeeping for all international transactions and partnerships', 'Research', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:12.881812', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Trading with the Enemy Act of 1917 (TWEA), codified at 50 U.S.C. §§ 4301-4341, grants the President extraordinary authority to regulate or prohibit any transactions in foreign exchange, banking, and financial dealings during times of war or national emergency. Originally enacted during World War I, TWEA remains one of the most powerful economic warfare statutes in U.S. law, directly impacting higher education institutions engaged in international research, partnerships, and financial transactions.

Under 50 U.S.C. § 4305(b), the President may investigate, regulate, or prohibit any transactions involving foreign countries or nationals thereof, including educational exchanges, research collaborations, and technology transfers. The Office of Foreign Assets Control (OFAC), operating under Treasury Department authority pursuant to 31 CFR Parts 500-598, implements TWEA sanctions programs that universities must navigate carefully.

Higher education institutions face significant compliance obligations under TWEA, particularly regarding research activities involving sanctioned countries or entities listed on OFAC''s Specially Designated Nationals (SDN) List. Universities conducting research with dual-use technologies must ensure compliance with both TWEA restrictions and Export Administration Regulations (EAR) under 15 CFR Parts 730-774. The intersection of these regulations creates complex compliance requirements for international research partnerships, student exchanges, and technology licensing agreements.

Key provisions affecting universities include prohibitions on transactions with blocked persons under 31 CFR § 501.201, mandatory blocking of assets under university control belonging to sanctioned entities, and restrictions on providing educational services that could benefit sanctioned countries'' strategic capabilities. Universities must implement robust screening procedures for students, researchers, visiting scholars, and institutional partners against OFAC sanctions lists.

Violations carry severe penalties under 50 U.S.C. § 4315, including criminal fines up to $1 million for organizations and civil penalties up to $307,922 per violation as of 2023 adjustments under 31 CFR § 501.805. The Treasury Department has assessed significant penalties against universities for TWEA violations, including $100,000 against University of Massachusetts for unlicensed transactions with Iranian students.

Recent enforcement actions demonstrate OFAC''s focus on higher education compliance. Universities must maintain comprehensive sanctions compliance programs including annual risk assessments, staff training, transaction monitoring systems, and detailed recordkeeping requirements under 31 CFR § 501.601. The complexity of modern university operations, including online education platforms serving global audiences, requires sophisticated compliance infrastructure to avoid inadvertent TWEA violations.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:12.881812', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-070', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

1. Contract only with eligible third-party servicers whose status has not been limited, suspended, or terminated by the Department of Education
2. Notify the Department within 10 business days of new contracts, substantial modifications, terminations, or servicer cessation of operations
3. Include mandatory contract provisions ensuring Department access to servicer records and compliance with federal regulations
4. Maintain ultimate responsibility for Title IV compliance regardless of services contracted to third parties
5. Terminate contracts immediately if servicer eligibility is compromised or compliance violations are identified', 'Financial Aid', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:12.90989', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Contracts with Third Party Servicers regulation, codified under 20 U.S.C. § 1094(c) and implemented through 34 CFR § 668.25, establishes mandatory oversight requirements for higher education institutions that engage external entities to administer any aspect of federal Title IV student financial aid programs. This comprehensive regulatory framework ensures institutional accountability while maintaining program integrity across all federal student aid operations.

Under 34 CFR § 668.25(a), institutions may only contract with third-party servicers whose eligibility remains intact and has not been limited, suspended, or terminated by the Department of Education. The regulation defines third-party servicers broadly to include any entity that contracts with an institution to administer, through either manual or automated processing, any aspect of Title IV programs, including application processing, need analysis, disbursement management, or student status reporting.

Key provisions under 34 CFR § 668.25(b) require institutions to provide written notification to the Department within 10 business days of: (1) executing new contracts or substantially modifying existing agreements; (2) contract terminations; or (3) servicer cessation of operations. Substantial modifications include changes to services provided, compensation structures, or administrative responsibilities that affect Title IV program operations.

The regulation mandates under 34 CFR § 668.25(c) that all contracts include specific provisions ensuring Department access to servicer records, compliance with federal regulations, and liability allocation between institutions and servicers. Institutions retain ultimate responsibility for Title IV compliance regardless of contracted services, as established in 20 U.S.C. § 1094(c)(1)(A).

Enforcement mechanisms include contract termination requirements, financial liability assessments, and potential limitation or suspension of institutional Title IV eligibility under 34 CFR § 668.86. The Department maintains authority to require immediate contract cessation if servicer eligibility is compromised or compliance violations are identified.

Recent updates through Federal Register Vol. 84, No. 209 strengthened oversight requirements, expanded notification obligations, and clarified institutional liability standards for servicer actions affecting Title IV program integrity and student aid delivery.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:12.90989', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-035', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

1. Establish written verification policies and procedures for FAFSA applications
2. Verify income, tax information, household size, and other specified data elements
3. Complete verification before disbursing Title IV funds with limited exceptions
4. Notify students of verification outcomes within 30 days
5. Maintain verification documentation for three years post-award year', 'Financial Aid', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:12.938431', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Higher Education Act Aid Application Verification requirements, codified under 20 U.S.C. § 1094 and implemented through 34 CFR § 668.56-668.61, mandate that all Title IV participating institutions establish comprehensive written policies and procedures for verifying information submitted on Free Application for Federal Student Aid (FAFSA) forms. This regulation stems from the Higher Education Opportunity Act of 2008 (Public Law 110-315) and serves as a critical fraud prevention mechanism in federal student aid administration.

Under 34 CFR § 668.57, institutions must verify applications selected by the Department of Education or those containing conflicting information. The verification process requires institutions to obtain documentation supporting income, tax information, household size, number in college, SNAP benefits, and other specified data elements. Institutions must complete verification before disbursing Title IV funds, with limited exceptions under 34 CFR § 668.60 for Pell Grant recipients in specific circumstances.

Key regulatory requirements include establishing timeframes for document submission, typically 120 days from notification or by the last day of the award year, whichever is earlier. Institutions must clearly communicate consequences for non-compliance, including aid suspension or cancellation. Under 34 CFR § 668.59, schools must notify students of verification outcomes within 30 days and provide correction procedures when discrepancies exceed tolerance thresholds established in 34 CFR § 668.58.

The regulation mandates referral procedures to the Department of Education''s Office of Inspector General when institutions suspect fraudulent activity under 34 CFR § 668.16(g). Institutions must maintain documentation for three years post-award year and ensure staff receive adequate training on verification procedures.

Penalties for non-compliance include fines, program limitation, suspension, or termination of Title IV eligibility under 34 CFR § 668.86. Recent updates through Federal Student Aid guidance emphasize streamlined verification processes and reduced documentation requirements for certain applicant categories, particularly those filing IRS Data Retrieval Tool information.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:12.938431', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-040', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

1. Arrange annual compliance audits by independent certified public accountants or qualified government auditors
2. Ensure audits examine Title IV program transactions, student eligibility determinations, and disbursement procedures
3. Submit completed audit reports to Department of Education within six months after fiscal year end
4. Maintain compliance with program participation agreements and cash management procedures
5. Cover all Title IV activities including Federal Pell Grants, Federal Direct Loans, and Federal Work-Study programs', 'Financial Aid', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:12.966331', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Higher Education Act Auditing Requirements, codified at 20 U.S.C. § 1094 and implemented through 34 CFR § 668.23, establish mandatory independent auditing standards for institutions participating in federal Title IV student financial assistance programs. These comprehensive audit requirements ensure institutional accountability and proper stewardship of federal education funds distributed through programs including Federal Pell Grants, Federal Direct Loans, and Federal Work-Study programs.

Under 34 CFR § 668.23(a), participating institutions must arrange for annual compliance audits conducted by independent certified public accountants or qualified government auditors. The audit must encompass two distinct components: (1) a compliance audit specifically examining the institution''s administration of Title IV programs, and (2) an audit of the institution''s general purpose financial statements. Audits conducted under the Office of Management and Budget''s Uniform Guidance (2 CFR Part 200, Subpart F) satisfy these regulatory requirements.

The compliance audit scope, detailed in 34 CFR § 668.23(c), must cover all Title IV program transactions and activities during the award year, including student eligibility determinations, disbursement procedures, return of Title IV funds calculations, enrollment reporting, and maintenance of required records. Auditors must specifically examine institutional compliance with program participation agreements, cohort default rate management, and cash management procedures as outlined in 34 CFR § 668.162.

Institutions must submit completed audit reports to the Department of Education within six months after the end of the fiscal year being audited, as specified in 34 CFR § 668.23(e). The Department utilizes these audits to monitor institutional compliance and identify systemic issues requiring corrective action. Audit findings may trigger additional oversight measures, including provisional certification or enhanced monitoring status under 34 CFR § 668.13.

Violations of audit requirements carry significant penalties under 20 U.S.C. § 1094(c)(3), including fines up to $27,500 per violation and potential suspension from Title IV programs. The Department may also require institutions to submit additional documentation, undergo more frequent audits, or post letters of credit to ensure continued program participation. Recent enforcement actions have emphasized timely submission and comprehensive coverage of all program activities, reflecting heightened federal oversight of institutional compliance with audit requirements.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:12.966331', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-041', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

1. Maintain comprehensive documentation of student recruitment, enrollment processes, and educational services
2. Implement systematic review processes for all student communications and marketing materials
3. Establish robust internal controls to prevent substantial misrepresentations about programs or employment outcomes
4. Provide staff training on permissible marketing practices and borrower defense regulations
5. Prepare for potential Department recovery proceedings through proper financial protection arrangements', 'Financial Aid', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:12.993522', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Higher Education Act''s Borrower Defense to Repayment provision, codified at 20 U.S.C. § 1087e(h) and implemented through 34 CFR § 685.222, provides eligible students with federal Direct Loans the right to seek loan discharge when their institution engaged in misconduct. This regulation operates under a bifurcated framework based on loan disbursement dates, creating distinct standards and procedures.

For loans first disbursed before July 1, 2017, students may assert borrower defense claims under the pre-2017 regulations (34 CFR § 685.222 as it existed prior to July 1, 2017). These claims require demonstrating that the institution committed an act or omission related to loan origination or educational service provision that would constitute a valid cause of action under applicable state law. The standard encompasses broader misconduct categories including fraud, misrepresentation, breach of contract, and violations of state consumer protection statutes.

For loans disbursed on or after July 1, 2017, the current 34 CFR § 685.222 establishes three specific grounds for borrower defense: (1) a final judgment from a court or administrative tribunal finding the school liable for violating applicable state law related to students'' federal student loans or education; (2) the school''s breach of contract; or (3) substantial misrepresentation by the school regarding the nature of the educational program, requirements for obtaining employment, or other factors that would reasonably cause the student to rely to their detriment.

Under 20 U.S.C. § 1087e(h)(2) and 34 CFR § 685.222(k), when the Department grants borrower defense relief, it may recover funds from the institution through several mechanisms. The Department can initiate collection proceedings, demand immediate payment, or pursue recovery through the institution''s surety or other financial protection arrangements required under 34 CFR Part 668. Recovery amounts typically equal the loan discharge amount plus accrued interest.

Institutions must maintain comprehensive documentation of student recruitment, enrollment processes, educational services, and financial aid administration to defend against potential borrower defense claims. The Department''s recovery authority extends to affiliated entities and successors under certain circumstances, as specified in 34 CFR § 685.222(l). Compliance requires robust internal controls, staff training on permissible marketing practices, and systematic review of student communications to prevent substantial misrepresentations that could trigger borrower defense liability.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:12.993522', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-042', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

 	Prohibits the payment of commissions or remuneration to anyone based on the value of a charitable gift annuity given to a public charity.', '', 'Fundraising & Development', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.760766', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Philanthropy Protection Act of 1995 (Public Law 104-62) establishes critical disclosure and operational requirements for charitable gift annuity programs operated by higher education institutions and other public charities. Codified at 26 U.S.C. § 501(m) and implemented through Treasury Regulation 26 CFR § 1.501(m)-1, this federal legislation addresses two fundamental aspects of charitable gift annuity administration: mandatory disclosure requirements and prohibition of commission-based compensation.

Under 26 U.S.C. § 501(m)(3), institutions must provide comprehensive disclosure statements to all existing annuitants within their Gift Annuity Fund and to all prospective donors at the point of initial solicitation. These disclosures must be delivered in written format, typically through formal letters or informational pamphlets, and must include specific information about annuity rates, payment terms, tax implications, and the institution''s financial backing of the annuity obligations.

The Act''s second major provision, codified at 26 U.S.C. § 501(m)(4), explicitly prohibits the payment of commissions, finder''s fees, or any form of performance-based remuneration to individuals based on the dollar value of charitable gift annuities secured for the institution. This prohibition extends to development officers, external consultants, financial advisors, and any third-party solicitors engaged by the university.

For higher education institutions, compliance is particularly critical as violations can result in loss of tax-exempt status under IRC Section 501(c)(3) and potential penalties under 26 U.S.C. § 4958 regarding excess benefit transactions. The Internal Revenue Service maintains enforcement authority and conducts periodic audits of gift annuity programs, with particular scrutiny of institutions processing more than $1 million annually in new gift annuities.

Institutions must maintain detailed records of all disclosure communications, documented evidence of timely delivery to donors and annuitants, and comprehensive documentation of compensation arrangements for all personnel involved in gift annuity solicitation and administration. The Act requires ongoing compliance monitoring and annual certification by institutional leadership regarding adherence to both disclosure and commission prohibition requirements.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.760766', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-249', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

Under 34 CFR § 668.162, institutions must maintain separate accountability for Title IV funds through proper cash management procedures. The Department of Education requires institutions to minimize the time between receipt of federal funds and disbursement to students, following the "minimize cash-on-hand" principle outlined in 34 CFR § 668.166. Institutions may request funds through the reimbursement, advance, or heightened cash monitoring payment methods, with specific eligibility criteria defined in 34 CFR § 668.162.

Key provisions include mandatory reconciliation procedures under 34 CFR § 668.16(h), requiring institutions to reconcile FSA program records within 60 days of the payment period end date. Institutions must maintain detailed financial records demonstrating compliance with federal cash management standards, including proper segregation of Title IV funds from institutional operating accounts. The regulations mandate specific disbursement timeframes, generally requiring institutions to disburse aid within 14 days of crediting student accounts for educational expenses.

Violations can result in severe enforcement actions under 20 U.S.C. § 1094(c), including placement on reimbursement payment method, fine assessments up to $55,000 per violation, or program participation suspension. Recent amendments through the FAFSA Simplification Act have modified certain cash management requirements effective for the 2024-25 award year, particularly regarding Professional Judgment documentation and verification procedures. Institutions must also comply with Federal Register guidance updates and Dear Colleague Letters that clarify cash management procedures and provide implementation timelines for regulatory changes.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.022805', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-043', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

Under 34 CFR § 668.14(b)(27), institutions must develop codes that explicitly prohibit conflicts of interest in all financial aid activities. The code must address revenue-sharing arrangements with lenders, preferential lending arrangements that benefit institutional personnel, staffing assistance from lenders, and gifts or other benefits provided by lending institutions. These provisions ensure that financial aid decisions serve student interests rather than institutional financial gain.

The regulation requires prominent publication of the code on the institution''s website, making it easily accessible to students, families, and oversight agencies. Additionally, 20 U.S.C. § 1094(a)(25)(B) mandates annual distribution to all personnel involved in financial aid administration, including financial aid officers, admissions staff, and institutional agents who interact with students regarding educational financing.

Compliance monitoring occurs through the Department of Education''s program reviews and audits. Violations may result in penalties under 20 U.S.C. § 1094(c), including fine assessments, limitation or termination of Title IV program participation, and required corrective action plans. The Department may impose civil monetary penalties up to $25,000 per violation for institutions that fail to maintain adequate codes of conduct.

Recent enforcement actions have emphasized the need for codes to address online education partnerships, third-party servicer relationships, and emerging financial aid technologies. Institutions must regularly review and update their codes to address evolving practices in higher education finance, ensuring continued compliance with federal requirements and protection of student interests in all financial aid processes.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.050962', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-245', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

1. Maintain cohort default rates below 30% for three consecutive years
2. Keep single-year default rates below 40% to retain loan program eligibility
3. Provide mandatory entrance and exit counseling to all borrowers
4. Monitor National Student Loan Data System (NSLDS) data for borrower repayment status
5. Respond to draft CDR calculations and submit challenges when necessary', 'Financial Aid', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.079761', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Higher Education Act Cohort Default Rate (CDR) provisions, codified at 20 U.S.C. § 1085 and implemented through 34 CFR § 668.214, establish critical institutional eligibility thresholds for participation in Title IV federal student aid programs. The CDR measures the percentage of borrowers who enter repayment on Federal Family Education Loan (FFEL) or Direct Loan programs during a specific federal fiscal year and default within specified timeframes.

Under 34 CFR § 668.202, institutions face progressive sanctions based on CDR thresholds. Schools with CDRs of 30% or higher for three consecutive years lose eligibility for Direct Loan, FFEL, and Pell Grant programs. Institutions exceeding 40% in any single year face immediate loss of Direct Loan and FFEL eligibility, though Pell Grant eligibility continues under 34 CFR § 668.206.

The Department of Education calculates CDRs using data from the National Student Loan Data System (NSLDS), examining borrowers who entered repayment during the federal fiscal year two years prior to the draft calculation. For example, the FY2024 CDR examines borrowers entering repayment in FY2022. Institutions receive draft CDR data annually and may challenge calculations through data corrections or appeals under 34 CFR § 668.209.

Key statutory authority derives from 20 U.S.C. § 1015a, requiring annual CDR publication, and 20 U.S.C. § 1085, establishing eligibility standards. Recent amendments through the CARES Act temporarily excluded certain COVID-19 related forbearances from default calculations for FY2020-2023 cohorts.

Enforcement mechanisms include provisional certification under 34 CFR § 668.16, requiring increased oversight and potentially shortened certification periods. Institutions may regain eligibility by demonstrating CDR improvement below thresholds for two consecutive years or by meeting fiscal responsibility standards with enhanced monitoring.

Institutions must implement default prevention strategies, including entrance and exit counseling per 34 CFR § 685.304, and may utilize default prevention consultants to maintain compliance. The regulation applies to all Title IV-eligible institutions with borrowers entering repayment, regardless of institutional type or size.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.079761', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-044', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

1. Obtain accreditation from a recognized accrediting agency
2. Secure authorization to operate in your state
3. Demonstrate administrative capability under federal standards
4. Meet financial responsibility requirements
5. Complete recertification following any ownership or control changes', 'Financial Aid', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.179314', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Higher Education Act Eligibility and Certification provisions, codified at 20 U.S.C. § 1099c and implemented through 34 CFR Part 600, establish fundamental prerequisites for institutional participation in federal Title IV student financial assistance programs. Under the Higher Education Opportunity Act of 2008 (Public Law 110-315), institutions must obtain and maintain Department of Education certification as eligible institutions to administer federal student aid programs including Pell Grants, Federal Direct Loans, and Federal Work-Study programs.

Eligible institutions must satisfy statutory requirements under 20 U.S.C. § 1001, including accreditation by a recognized accrediting agency, authorization to operate in their state, and demonstration of administrative capability under 34 CFR § 668.16. The Secretary of Education evaluates institutional eligibility based on academic standards, financial responsibility standards under 34 CFR Part 668 Subpart B, and administrative capability requirements.

Change in ownership provisions under 20 U.S.C. § 1099c-1 and 34 CFR § 600.31 require institutions undergoing ownership changes resulting in control changes to recertify eligibility. Following such changes, both the chief administrator and designated Title IV administrator must complete comprehensive program training within 12 months of executing the new program participation agreement under 34 CFR § 668.16(a). This training covers regulatory compliance, financial management, student eligibility determinations, and program administration requirements.

Enforcement mechanisms include suspension or termination of program participation under 34 CFR § 668.86, fine assessments, and limitation on administrative capability findings. The Department may impose provisional certification under 34 CFR § 668.13 for institutions demonstrating compliance deficiencies. Recent amendments through the FAFSA Simplification Act have enhanced oversight requirements and modified administrative capability standards effective July 2023.

Institutions must maintain continuous compliance with eligibility criteria, submit annual compliance audits under 34 CFR Part 668 Subpart E, and report material changes in organizational structure, ownership, or control within 10 days under 34 CFR § 600.21. Non-compliance may result in program termination, financial liability for improperly disbursed funds, and exclusion from future Title IV participation.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.179314', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-045', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

1. Provide entrance counseling to first-time federal loan borrowers before disbursement covering loan terms, interest rates, fees, and borrower responsibilities
2. Deliver exit counseling within 30 days when students graduate, withdraw, or drop below half-time enrollment
3. Include comprehensive information on repayment options, loan servicer contacts, default consequences, and deferment programs
4. Maintain documentation of counseling completion for federal compliance reviews and audits
5. Ensure counseling materials remain current with federal regulations and provide accurate borrower information', 'Financial Aid', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.207623', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Higher Education Act''s Entrance and Exit Counseling requirements under 20 U.S.C. § 1092(b) and (l), implemented through 34 CFR § 685.304, mandate comprehensive loan counseling obligations for institutions participating in federal student aid programs. Section 488 of the Higher Education Opportunity Act (Public Law 110-315) established detailed counseling requirements to ensure borrowers understand their loan obligations and available options.

Entrance counseling, required under 20 U.S.C. § 1092(b), must be provided to first-time federal loan borrowers before loan disbursement. Institutions must deliver comprehensive information including loan terms, interest rates, fees, borrower rights and responsibilities, consequences of default, and debt management strategies. The counseling must be provided through in-person sessions, signed written materials, or interactive online programs that test borrower comprehension, as specified in 34 CFR § 685.304(a).

Exit counseling requirements under 20 U.S.C. § 1092(l) apply when borrowers cease enrollment below half-time status, graduate, or withdraw. Institutions must provide detailed information on repayment options, loan servicer contact information, consequences of delinquency and default, loan consolidation options, forbearance and deferment programs, and tax implications of loan forgiveness. The Department of Education''s regulations at 34 CFR § 685.304(b) require completion within 30 days of the triggering event.

Institutions must maintain documentation of counseling completion for federal compliance reviews and audits. Non-compliance can result in program review findings, financial liability, and potential loss of Title IV eligibility under 34 CFR § 668.14. The Department''s Federal Student Aid office conducts regular monitoring through program reviews and student complaints to ensure institutional adherence to counseling requirements.

Recent updates through the FUTURE Act and regulatory changes have enhanced online counseling capabilities and standardized content requirements. Institutions must ensure counseling materials remain current with federal regulations and provide borrowers with accurate, comprehensive information to support informed borrowing decisions and successful loan repayment.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.207623', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-046', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

Under § 8B2.1(b), universities must establish comprehensive compliance programs featuring seven essential elements: (1) compliance standards and procedures capable of reducing criminal conduct; (2) oversight by high-level personnel with sufficient authority and resources; (3) due care in delegating substantial discretionary authority; (4) effective communication of standards through training and publications; (5) reasonable monitoring and auditing systems; (6) consistent enforcement including disciplinary mechanisms; and (7) reasonable response to detected violations.

The legal authority extends through 28 U.S.C. Chapter 58, which governs the United States Sentencing Commission''s regulatory authority. Universities face vicarious liability for criminal acts committed by agents, employees, or students acting within their scope of authority. This includes violations of federal research regulations, Title IX violations with criminal elements, financial aid fraud, and export control violations.

Enforcement mechanisms under 18 U.S.C. § 3571 include organizational fines up to $500 million for felonies, with mitigation factors available for institutions demonstrating effective compliance programs per § 8C2.5. The 2010 amendments to § 8B2.1 strengthened requirements for periodic risk assessments and enhanced training protocols, while 2018 revisions emphasized data analytics and confidential reporting mechanisms.

Higher education institutions must integrate these requirements with existing regulatory frameworks including the Clery Act (20 U.S.C. § 1092(f)), Title IX (20 U.S.C. § 1681), and research integrity standards under 2 CFR Part 200. The guidelines'' effectiveness is measured through reduced recidivism and demonstrated organizational commitment to ethical conduct, with sentencing courts evaluating compliance program adequacy during criminal proceedings involving institutional defendants.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.789564', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-176', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

1. Execute and maintain a Participation Agreement with the Department of Education
2. Contribute at least 25% institutional matching funds for each FSEOG grant awarded
3. Maintain separate accounting systems for federal funds with internal controls preventing single-office authorization
4. Prioritize Pell Grant recipients and students with exceptional financial need in award decisions
5. Submit annual Fiscal Operations Report and Application to Participate (FISAP) for accountability and funding requests', 'Financial Aid', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.236104', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Federal Supplemental Educational Opportunity Grant (FSEOG) Program, codified under 20 U.S.C. §§ 1070b-1070b-4 and regulated by 34 CFR Part 676, provides need-based grant assistance to exceptionally needy undergraduate students. This campus-based program requires institutions to contribute matching funds and exercise significant administrative responsibilities to ensure compliance with federal requirements.

Under 20 U.S.C. § 1070b-2(a), institutions must execute a Participation Agreement with the Department of Education establishing their commitment to program requirements. The statutory framework mandates strict fund management protocols under 34 CFR § 668.163, requiring institutions to maintain separate accounting for federal funds and implement internal controls preventing single-office authorization and disbursement of payments, as specified in 34 CFR § 676.19.

The matching requirement under 20 U.S.C. § 1070b-2(b) obligates institutions to contribute at least 25% of each grant awarded, creating a federal-institutional partnership in student aid delivery. Annual grants range from $100 to $4,000 per student, with priority selection criteria favoring Pell Grant recipients and students with exceptional financial need as defined in 34 CFR § 676.10.

Fund transfer restrictions under 20 U.S.C. § 1070b-3 historically prohibited movement of FSEOG allocations to other programs. However, the Higher Education Opportunity Act of 2008 (Public Law 110-315) amended these provisions, permitting institutions to transfer up to 25% of their FSEOG allocation to Federal Work-Study programs under specific circumstances outlined in 34 CFR § 676.18.

Compliance obligations include maintaining comprehensive program and fiscal records under 34 CFR § 676.19, managing carry-forward and carry-back provisions within statutory limits of 10% annually, and adhering to eligibility determinations based on Expected Family Contribution calculations. The annual Fiscal Operations Report and Application to Participate (FISAP) submission, required under 34 CFR § 676.17, serves as both accountability mechanism and allocation request for subsequent award years.

Enforcement mechanisms include program review audits, fund recovery procedures for non-compliance, and potential termination of institutional participation for severe violations. Recent regulatory updates have emphasized enhanced oversight of institutional matching contributions and stricter documentation requirements for student eligibility determinations, reflecting increased federal scrutiny of campus-based aid programs.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.236104', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-047', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

1. Contribute minimum 25% non-federal share of all student wages with federal government providing remaining 75%
2. Allocate at least 7% of total FWS funding for community service positions including one reading tutor role
3. Pay federal minimum wage ($7.25/hour) or applicable state minimum wage, whichever is higher
4. Restrict student employment during non-enrollment periods to those planning to re-enroll in subsequent academic period
5. Prohibit private for-profit organizations from participating as community service employers', 'Financial Aid', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.264814', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Federal Work Study (FWS) Program, established under Title IV of the Higher Education Act of 1965 as amended by the Higher Education Opportunity Act (Public Law 110-315, 42 U.S.C. §§ 2751-2757), provides part-time employment opportunities for undergraduate and graduate students with demonstrated financial need. This need-based federal aid program enables students to earn money to help pay educational expenses while gaining valuable work experience.

Under 34 CFR § 675.18, participating institutions must contribute a minimum non-federal share of 25% of student wages, with the federal government providing the remaining 75%. The total compensation must meet Fair Labor Standards Act requirements (29 U.S.C. § 206), paying federal minimum wage ($7.25/hour) or applicable state minimum wage, whichever is higher. Volunteered or unpaid work hours cannot count toward FWS employment under any circumstances (34 CFR § 675.20).

A critical statutory requirement mandates institutions use at least 7% of their total FWS allocation for community service positions (42 U.S.C. § 2756a). Within this allocation, institutions must employ at least one student as a reading tutor for children, particularly in elementary schools or family literacy projects. Private for-profit organizations are explicitly prohibited from participating as community service employers under 34 CFR § 675.19(a).

Student eligibility during non-enrollment periods requires careful compliance monitoring. Students may continue FWS employment during breaks or between terms only if they plan to enroll in the subsequent academic period (34 CFR § 675.16(a)). If a student fails to re-enroll, institutions must demonstrate reasonable belief that the student intended to return, based on documentation such as advance registration, housing deposits, or written enrollment commitments.

Title III (Strengthening Institutions) and Title V (Hispanic-Serving Institutions) designated institutions may qualify for waivers of the 25% non-federal share requirement for both FWS and Federal Supplemental Educational Opportunity Grant programs (20 U.S.C. §§ 1057-1059g, 1101a-1103g). Institutions must submit annual "Application for Designation as an Eligible Institution" through the Department of Education''s grants management system.

Enforcement mechanisms include program review audits, fund recapture for non-compliance, and potential termination of institutional participation. The Department of Education conducts regular monitoring through Federal Student Aid''s oversight protocols, with institutions required to maintain detailed payroll records, student eligibility documentation, and community service tracking reports for federal examination.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.264814', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-048', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

1. Maintain composite score above 1.5 using primary reserve ratio, equity ratio, and net income ratio calculations
2. Submit audited financial statements prepared according to generally accepted accounting principles
3. Report within 10 days any events affecting financial responsibility including equity withdrawals exceeding 25%
4. Demonstrate sufficient financial resources to provide educational services and meet all commitments
5. Maintain administrative capability to manage Title IV programs effectively', 'Financial Aid', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.292969', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Higher Education Act''s Financial Responsibility Requirements, codified at 20 U.S.C. § 1094 and 20 U.S.C. § 1099c, establish mandatory financial standards for institutions participating in federal Title IV student aid programs. Under 34 CFR § 668.15, institutions must demonstrate financial responsibility through specific metrics including a composite score derived from primary reserve ratio, equity ratio, and net income ratio calculations.

The Department of Education evaluates financial responsibility using audited financial statements prepared according to generally accepted accounting principles. Institutions scoring below 1.5 on the composite score are deemed not financially responsible and face heightened oversight under 34 CFR § 668.174. Those scoring between 1.5-2.6 are in the oversight zone requiring additional monitoring.

Key provisions under 34 CFR § 668.171 mandate institutions maintain sufficient financial resources to provide educational services, meet debts and commitments, and provide required services to students. Institutions must demonstrate administrative capability to manage Title IV programs effectively and maintain adequate systems for financial management and student records.

Notification requirements under 20 U.S.C. § 1099c and 34 CFR § 668.174 compel institutions to report within 10 days any events affecting financial responsibility, including: withdrawal of owner''s equity exceeding 25% of total equity, litigation involving potential liability exceeding 10% of institution''s equity, loss of institutional accreditation candidacy status, or adverse actions by state licensing agencies.

Enforcement mechanisms include provisional certification under 34 CFR § 668.13, requiring institutions to post letters of credit up to 50% of prior year Title IV funds received. The Department may terminate program participation for institutions failing to meet financial responsibility standards under 34 CFR § 668.86.

Recent amendments through the 2022 Federal Student Aid Handbook clarified composite score calculations for proprietary institutions and updated reporting thresholds for ownership changes. The Department enhanced monitoring procedures for institutions experiencing significant enrollment declines or revenue fluctuations exceeding 25% year-over-year under current 34 CFR § 668.174 provisions.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.292969', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-049', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

1. Distribute annual notices covering financial assistance programs, costs, and refund policies
2. Provide student outcome data including retention, graduation, and transfer rates by demographics
3. Publish annual campus security reports with crime statistics and safety policies
4. Disclose athletics equity data for institutions with intercollegiate programs
5. Maintain FERPA notices informing students of education record rights', 'Financial Aid', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.321128', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Higher Education Act (HEA) Information Distribution Requirements, codified at 20 U.S.C. § 1092 and implemented through 34 CFR § 668.41-668.49, mandate comprehensive annual disclosure obligations for all Title IV-eligible postsecondary institutions. This federal regulation serves as a cornerstone of consumer protection in higher education, ensuring prospective and enrolled students receive critical information necessary for informed educational decisions.

Under 20 U.S.C. § 1092(a)(1), institutions must annually distribute notices of availability covering seven distinct categories of information. First, financial assistance disclosures must detail all federal, state, local, private, and institutional aid programs, including eligibility requirements, application procedures, and award determination methods pursuant to 34 CFR § 668.42. Second, comprehensive institutional information must encompass cost of attendance calculations per 34 CFR § 668.43, including tuition, fees, room, board, books, supplies, transportation, and personal expenses, alongside refund policies under 34 CFR § 668.22.

Academic program descriptions, faculty qualifications, physical facilities, and services for disabled students must comply with Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act and the Americans with Disabilities Act. Withdrawal procedures must align with 34 CFR § 668.22 Return to Title IV requirements. Third, student outcome data under 34 CFR § 668.45 requires disclosure of retention rates, graduation rates within 150% of normal program time, and transfer-out rates disaggregated by gender, race, ethnicity, and Federal Pell Grant recipient status.

Fourth, employment placement and graduate school enrollment data must be provided for degree or certificate programs designed to prepare students for gainful employment, as specified in 34 CFR § 668.412. Fifth, the annual security report mandated by the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (20 U.S.C. § 1092(f)) must include campus crime statistics, security policies, and procedures for reporting criminal actions.

Sixth, institutions with intercollegiate athletic programs must provide equity in athletics data per the EADA, detailing participation rates and financial support by gender under 20 U.S.C. § 1092(e). Seventh, FERPA notices under 20 U.S.C. § 1232g and 34 CFR Part 99 must inform students of their rights to inspect, review, and request amendment of education records.

Per 34 CFR § 668.41(c), institutions may provide notices through direct distribution, posting on institutional websites with prominent links, or other electronic means ensuring reasonable access. All information must be made available to prospective students upon request under 20 U.S.C. § 1092(a)(4). Non-compliance may result in fine, limitation, suspension, or termination of Title IV program participation under 34 CFR § 668.83, with penalties up to $55,771 per violation as of 2023 adjustments under the Federal Civil Penalties Inflation Adjustment Act.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.321128', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-050', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

1. Display prominent net price calculator on primary website using DOE template
2. Include comprehensive cost data covering tuition, fees, room, board, books, and other expenses
3. Update calculator annually with institution-specific financial aid data by income categories
4. Submit explanatory reports within 30 days if listed for high tuition increases
5. Maintain compliance with federal consumer information disclosure requirements', 'Financial Aid', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.350776', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Higher Education Act Net Price Calculator requirements, codified at 20 U.S.C. § 1015a and implemented through 34 CFR § 668.6, mandate that all institutions participating in Title IV federal student aid programs maintain transparent pricing information through standardized calculators and cost reporting mechanisms.

Under 20 U.S.C. § 1015a(a)(1), any institution receiving Title IV funds must prominently display a net price calculator on its primary website that enables current and prospective students to estimate their individual net price of attendance. The calculator must use the template developed by the Department of Education and include comprehensive cost data encompassing tuition, fees, room, board, books, supplies, transportation, and other expenses as defined in 34 CFR § 668.2.

The net price calculator must incorporate institution-specific data including average amounts of federal, state, institutional, and other financial aid awarded to students in different income categories, disaggregated by dependency status and family income levels pursuant to 20 U.S.C. § 1015a(b). Institutions must update calculator data annually using the most recent cohort information available, typically from the prior academic year''s enrolled students.

Additionally, 20 U.S.C. § 1015a(f) establishes enhanced reporting requirements for institutions appearing on the Department''s annual list of schools with the highest percentage increases in tuition and fees or net price over the preceding three academic years. These institutions must submit detailed explanatory reports within 30 days of notification, documenting specific reasons for cost increases and concrete remediation strategies to control future price growth.

The Department of Education enforces compliance through its Federal Student Aid office, with violations potentially resulting in program review activities, provisional certification, or loss of Title IV eligibility under 34 CFR § 668.13. The Secretary may impose administrative actions against non-compliant institutions, including fines and restrictions on federal funding disbursement.

Recent updates through the FUTURE Act of 2019 enhanced data sharing capabilities and streamlined FAFSA processes, while maintaining core net price calculator obligations. Institutions must ensure calculators reflect current pricing models and incorporate all mandatory disclosures required by federal consumer information regulations under 34 CFR § 668.41.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.350776', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-051', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

1. Verify parent citizenship status and Social Security numbers through NSLDS
2. Confirm borrowers do not have adverse credit histories including 90+ day delinquencies or defaults
3. Maintain current Cost of Attendance calculations and verify all other financial aid
4. Process electronic disbursements directly to institutional accounts with 14-day credit balance returns
5. Provide entrance counseling and maintain Master Promissory Note records', 'Financial Aid', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.37885', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Higher Education Act PLUS Loan Program, codified under 20 U.S.C. § 1078-2 and implemented through 34 CFR § 685.203, provides federal credit-based loans to parents of dependent undergraduate students and graduate/professional students. Established under the Higher Education Opportunity Act of 2008 (Public Law No. 110-315), PLUS loans are administered through the William D. Ford Federal Direct Loan Program, following the discontinuation of the Federal Family Education Loan (FFEL) Program in 2010.

Under 20 U.S.C. § 1087e, institutions must verify borrower eligibility through comprehensive documentation requirements. For Parent PLUS loans, universities must verify the parent''s citizenship status per 20 U.S.C. § 1091 and Social Security number through the Department of Education''s National Student Loan Data System (NSLDS). Credit history verification is mandatory under 34 CFR § 685.203(c), requiring institutions to confirm that borrowers do not have adverse credit histories as defined by 90+ day delinquencies, default determinations, bankruptcy discharge, foreclosure, repossession, tax lien, wage garnishment, or write-off of federal student aid debt within five years.

Loan amounts are governed by 20 U.S.C. § 1087e(a)(2), limiting borrowing to the student''s cost of attendance minus other financial assistance. Institutions must maintain current Cost of Attendance calculations per 20 U.S.C. § 1087ll and verify all other aid through NSLDS queries. Graduate PLUS loans follow identical terms under 34 CFR § 685.203(d), with borrowing limits calculated similarly.

Disbursement procedures under 34 CFR § 685.303 require electronic funds transfer directly to institutional accounts, with any credit balances returned to borrowers within 14 days per 34 CFR § 685.305(c). Schools must maintain Master Promissory Note records and provide entrance counseling per 20 U.S.C. § 1092(l).

Non-compliance penalties include program suspension, limitation, or termination under 20 U.S.C. § 1094, with potential liability for improperly certified loans. The Department of Education conducts program reviews under 34 CFR § 668.23, with findings potentially resulting in monetary penalties up to statutory maximums or loss of Title IV eligibility.

Recent updates include interest rate modifications effective July 1, 2023, and enhanced credit counseling requirements under the FAFSA Simplification Act provisions taking effect for 2024-2025 award year.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.37885', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-052', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

 	The application must contain all technical information, including but not limited to transmitting and receiving station names, coordinates, equipment, antennae information, etc.

 	Must demonstrate substantial service at the time of license renewal, and must provide a description of current service.', '', 'Information Technology', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:14.314851', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Federal Communications Commission (FCC) regulates Fixed Microwave Services licensing under 47 U.S.C. § 151 and implementing regulations found in 47 CFR Parts 1, 21, and 101. This comprehensive regulatory framework governs universities and higher education institutions that operate Digital Electronic Message Service (DEMS) Nodal Stations for campus communications, research facilities, or inter-campus connectivity.

Under 47 CFR § 101.21, universities must file Form 601 applications electronically through the Universal Licensing System (ULS) for each fixed microwave facility. The Communications Act of 1934, specifically 47 U.S.C. § 310(a), prohibits foreign government ownership or control of radio station licenses, making universities with foreign government affiliations ineligible for licensing.

Technical specifications required under 47 CFR § 101.31 include precise geographic coordinates using North American Datum 1983 (NAD83), transmitter power output, antenna specifications including gain patterns and radiation characteristics, frequency coordination data, and interference analysis studies. Universities must also provide detailed path profiles and Fresnel zone clearance calculations per 47 CFR § 101.103.

License terms are typically ten years under 47 CFR § 1.949, with renewal applications due between 90 and 30 days before expiration per 47 CFR § 1.949(a). The substantial service requirement under 47 CFR § 101.67 mandates universities demonstrate meaningful usage patterns, not merely token operations, throughout the license term. Renewal applications must include detailed service descriptions, usage statistics, and operational history.

Enforcement mechanisms include monetary forfeitures up to $100,000 per violation under 47 U.S.C. § 503(b)(2)(B), license revocation, and construction permit cancellation. The FCC''s Enforcement Bureau actively monitors spectrum usage and investigates interference complaints. Recent amendments in 2019 streamlined certain technical requirements while maintaining strict foreign ownership restrictions under 47 CFR § 1.5000 series rules.

Universities must coordinate frequencies through certified frequency coordinators per 47 CFR § 101.103(d) and maintain detailed station records including modulation parameters, emission designators, and antenna registration numbers when required under 47 CFR Part 17 for towers exceeding 200 feet.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:14.314851', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-109', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

1. Provide written notification to all students at enrollment about drug conviction penalties
2. Implement systematic procedures to identify students who lose Title IV aid eligibility
3. Send secondary notifications to affected students detailing restoration pathways
4. Maintain records of all notifications and student acknowledgments
5. Establish processes for students to regain eligibility through rehabilitation programs', 'Financial Aid', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.406597', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Higher Education Act''s drug violation penalties provision, codified at 20 U.S.C. § 1092(k) and implemented through 34 CFR § 668.40, establishes mandatory disclosure requirements and eligibility restrictions for students convicted of drug-related offenses while receiving federal financial aid. This regulation directly impacts Title IV aid programs, including Federal Pell Grants, Federal Supplemental Educational Opportunity Grants, Federal Work-Study, Federal Perkins Loans, and Federal Family Education Loans.

Under 20 U.S.C. § 1092(k)(1), institutions must provide written notification to all students at enrollment informing them that any conviction for a drug offense during a period of enrollment for which Title IV aid is received will result in immediate loss of eligibility for all Title IV assistance. The statute specifically covers convictions under federal or state law involving possession or sale of illegal drugs, with suspension periods varying based on offense type and frequency.

For first-time possession offenses, students lose eligibility for one year from conviction date. Second possession offenses result in two-year suspensions, while third offenses cause indefinite ineligibility. Sale convictions carry more severe penalties: first offense results in two-year suspension, while second offense causes permanent ineligibility loss.

Institutions must implement systematic procedures under 34 CFR § 668.40(b) to identify affected students and provide secondary notifications to those who have lost eligibility. These notices must detail restoration pathways, including completion of qualified drug rehabilitation programs or passing two unannounced drug tests administered by approved programs.

The Department of Education''s enforcement mechanisms include program reviews, audit exceptions, and potential Title IV program termination for non-compliant institutions. Recent guidance from Federal Student Aid emphasizes digital notification acceptability while maintaining documentation requirements. Institutions must maintain records of all notifications and student acknowledgments for federal audit purposes.

Compliance failures can result in monetary liabilities, heightened cash monitoring status, or loss of Title IV authorization. The regulation''s scope extends to all degree-granting and certificate-granting institutions participating in federal financial aid programs, regardless of institutional size or sector.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.406597', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-053', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

1. Award loans only to students with exceptional financial need who have exhausted Federal Direct Loan eligibility
2. Provide comprehensive loan counseling and detailed disclosure information to all borrowers
3. Enter cooperative agreements with credit reporting agencies for borrower information exchange
4. Maintain program operations using only institutional funds and existing borrower collections
5. Comply with loan availability restrictions and wind-down provisions for new disbursements', 'Financial Aid', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.434846', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Federal Perkins Loan Program, established under Title IV of the Higher Education Act of 1965 (20 U.S.C. §§ 1087aa-1087ii), was a campus-based student loan program that provided low-interest loans to undergraduate and graduate students with exceptional financial need. Following the Higher Education Opportunity Act of 2008 (Public Law No. 110-315), the program has undergone significant changes and restrictions.

Under 20 U.S.C. § 1087aa(b), authorization for additional federal appropriations to the Federal Perkins Loan program beyond fiscal year 2015 is explicitly prohibited. This means institutions can only make new loans using existing institutional funds and collections from previous borrowers, making the program entirely self-sustaining without federal capital contributions.

Institutions participating in the program must comply with stringent requirements under 20 U.S.C. § 1087dd. First, loans must be awarded primarily to students with exceptional financial need, as defined in 20 U.S.C. § 1087bb. Second, institutions must provide comprehensive loan counseling and information to all borrowers as specified in 20 U.S.C. § 1087dd(c). Third, participating institutions must enter cooperative agreements with credit reporting agencies to exchange borrower information pursuant to 20 U.S.C. § 1087cc(c)(8).

Comprehensive disclosure requirements are mandated under 20 U.S.C. § 1087dd(c)(1), requiring institutions to provide borrowers with detailed loan information including: institutional name and payment address, principal amount and interest rate, all fees and charges, annual and cumulative borrowing limits, repayment terms and conditions, default consequences, consolidation options, prepayment rights, deferment provisions, and impact on other financial aid eligibility.

Loan availability restrictions under the program''s wind-down provisions specify that undergraduate loans could only be made through September 30, 2016, provided students had exhausted Federal Direct Loan eligibility. Graduate students could receive loans through March 31, 2018, only if they previously received Perkins loans for academic years ending before October 1, 2016, and had maximized Direct Loan eligibility as outlined in 20 U.S.C. § 1087bb(b).

Enforcement mechanisms include program participation suspension, fine assessment, and liability for federal fund recovery under 20 U.S.C. § 1087hh. The Department of Education conducts program reviews to ensure institutional compliance with federal requirements and proper fund management. Non-compliance may result in immediate program termination and institutional liability for outstanding federal capital contributions.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.434846', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-054', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

 	The university must disclose to borrowers the method and criteria used to select preferred lenders, and offer comparative information to borrowers about each lender''s benefits.

 	The university must include in its preferred lender materials a prominent statement that borrowers are not required to use any lender on the list, and may not delay award certification because a preferred lender is not used.

 	The preferred lender list and associated information must be updated annually.', '', 'Financial Aid', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.46598', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Higher Education Act''s Preferred Lender provisions, codified at 20 U.S.C. § 1094(h) and implemented through Public Law No. 110-315, establish comprehensive requirements for institutions of higher education that maintain preferred lender lists for student loan programs. These regulations were enacted as part of the Higher Education Opportunity Act of 2008 to enhance transparency and prevent conflicts of interest in student lending practices.

Under 20 U.S.C. § 1094(h)(1), institutions may maintain preferred lender lists provided they comply with strict operational requirements. The statute mandates that such lists must include at least three unaffiliated lenders and cannot be used to restrict or impede borrower choice in lender selection. Critically, 20 U.S.C. § 1094(h)(2) prohibits institutions from including lenders that have provided financial incentives, gifts, or other benefits in exchange for preferred status.

Transparency requirements under 20 U.S.C. § 1094(h)(3) mandate that institutions disclose their selection methodology and criteria to all borrowers. Institutions must provide comparative information regarding each preferred lender''s loan terms, benefits, and borrower services. The statute requires prominent disclosure that borrowers are not obligated to select from the preferred list and may choose any eligible lender.

Enforcement mechanisms are outlined in 20 U.S.C. § 1094(c), which authorizes the Department of Education to impose sanctions including fines, limitation of Title IV eligibility, or termination of federal student aid programs for non-compliance. Violations may result in civil monetary penalties up to $27,500 per violation under 34 CFR § 668.83.

The regulation specifically prohibits certification delays when borrowers select non-preferred lenders, as established in 20 U.S.C. § 1094(h)(4). Annual updates to preferred lender lists and associated materials are mandatory, ensuring current and accurate information for borrower decision-making. Recent amendments through the CARES Act and subsequent appropriations have maintained these core requirements while expanding oversight mechanisms for institutional compliance monitoring.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.46598', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-055', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

1. Maintain complete records of institutional eligibility and program authorization documentation
2. Document all student financial aid applications, disbursements, and eligibility determinations
3. Retain Title IV program records for three years following award year end
4. Preserve loan program records for three years after student''s last attendance
5. Keep all records under audit or investigation until final resolution', 'Financial Aid', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.493898', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Higher Education Act Record Retention requirements, codified in 34 CFR § 668.24, establish comprehensive documentation and record-keeping obligations for institutions participating in federal student aid programs under Title IV of the Higher Education Act (20 U.S.C. § 1070 et seq.). These regulations ensure proper administration, oversight, and accountability of federal education funding programs.

**Legal Authority and Scope**
Derived from the Higher Education Act of 1965 as amended (20 U.S.C. § 1094), these requirements apply to all postsecondary institutions participating in Title IV programs, including Federal Pell Grants, Federal Work-Study (FWS), Federal Supplemental Educational Opportunity Grants (FSEOG), Federal Perkins Loans, Federal Family Education Loans (FFEL), and Direct Loans (34 CFR § 668.1).

**Core Documentation Requirements**
Institutions must maintain comprehensive records documenting: (1) institutional eligibility and program authorization under 34 CFR § 600.4; (2) compliance with administrative requirements per 34 CFR Part 668; (3) financial responsibility standards under 34 CFR § 668.15; (4) complete application data for all program funds; and (5) accurate disbursement and delivery documentation per 34 CFR § 668.164.

**Student Financial Aid Records**
Specific documentation includes Student Aid Reports (SARs) or Institutional Student Information Records (ISIRs), all application data submitted for students or parent borrowers, comprehensive eligibility and receipt documentation, entrance and exit counseling records per 34 CFR § 685.304, federal reports and supporting verification data, and completion/graduation rate calculations under 34 CFR § 668.45.

**Retention Periods**
General Title IV records (Pell, FSEOG, FWS, Perkins) must be retained for three years following the award year end (34 CFR § 668.24(e)). Direct Loan and FFEL student records require three-year retention after the student''s last attendance, while other loan program records must be kept three years after submission to the Department of Education. Records subject to audit, investigation, or dispute resolution must be retained until final resolution, regardless of standard retention periods.

**Enforcement and Penalties**
Non-compliance may result in program suspension, termination of Title IV eligibility, financial liability for improperly awarded funds, and civil penalties under 34 CFR § 668.83. The Department of Education conducts regular program reviews and audits to ensure compliance with these retention requirements.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.493898', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-057', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

1. Maintain accurate student enrollment and academic progress records for all Title IV recipients
2. Ensure proper Return to Title IV (R2T4) calculations and timely fund disbursements
3. Implement robust internal controls for cash management and fund reconciliations
4. Establish written policies addressing conflicts of interest and preferred lender arrangements
5. Conduct annual third-party audits and maintain comprehensive audit trails for all transactions', 'Financial Aid', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.521594', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Higher Education Act (HEA) Standard of Conduct establishes fiduciary obligations for institutions participating in Title IV federal student aid programs under 20 U.S.C. § 1094(a)(3) and 34 CFR § 668.14. This regulation requires universities to administer federal financial aid programs with the highest standard of care and diligence, creating stringent accountability measures for institutional stewardship of federal funds.

Under 20 U.S.C. § 1094, institutions assume fiduciary responsibility for all Title IV funds, including Pell Grants, Federal Direct Loans, Federal Work-Study, and SEOG programs. The fiduciary standard demands meticulous administration, accurate recordkeeping, proper fund disbursement timing, and strict adherence to all program requirements as outlined in 34 CFR Parts 668-690.

Key fiduciary obligations include: maintaining accurate student enrollment and academic progress records; ensuring proper Return to Title IV (R2T4) calculations under 34 CFR § 668.22; implementing robust internal controls for cash management; conducting timely fund reconciliations; and maintaining comprehensive audit trails for all transactions. Institutions must establish written policies addressing conflicts of interest, particularly regarding preferred lender arrangements under 20 U.S.C. § 1094(a)(25).

The Department of Education enforces compliance through multiple mechanisms under 34 CFR § 668.86. Violations can trigger emergency actions including immediate fund suspension, civil monetary penalties up to $27,500 per violation under 20 U.S.C. § 1094(c)(3)(B), or program participation limitations. The 2008 Higher Education Opportunity Act (Public Law 110-315) strengthened enforcement provisions and increased penalty amounts, reflecting heightened congressional concern over institutional accountability.

Institutions must implement comprehensive compliance systems including annual third-party audits under 34 CFR § 668.23, regular staff training programs, and documented quality assurance procedures. The fiduciary standard applies institution-wide, encompassing financial aid offices, business offices, registrar operations, and senior administration. Failure to meet these obligations jeopardizes institutional eligibility for all federal student aid programs, potentially affecting thousands of students and millions in federal funding.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.521594', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-058', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

1. Monitor and maintain cohort default rates below statutory thresholds (25% for three consecutive years or 40% in any single year)
2. Provide mandatory entrance and exit counseling for all federal loan recipients
3. Implement default management plans if rates reach 20-24.9% range
4. Submit annual compliance reports and maintain comprehensive borrower communication records
5. Provide debt management education and career placement assistance to students', 'Financial Aid', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.549121', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Student Loan Default Prevention Initiative Act of 1990, codified at 20 U.S.C. §§ 1085(a) and 1094(c), establishes comprehensive measures to prevent excessive default rates on federal student loans and ensures institutional accountability in Title IV program administration. Under 34 CFR § 668.206, institutions with cohort default rates exceeding 25% for three consecutive award years, or 40% in any single award year, face immediate suspension from Federal Direct Loan, Federal Family Education Loan (FFEL), and Federal Pell Grant programs.

The regulatory framework requires institutions to calculate three-year cohort default rates based on borrowers who enter repayment during a specific federal fiscal year and default within the subsequent three years, as defined in 34 CFR § 668.202. The Department of Education annually publishes official cohort default rates, with institutions receiving draft rates for challenge opportunities under 34 CFR § 668.209 before final publication.

Key enforcement mechanisms include provisional certification under 34 CFR § 668.16(m), requiring institutions with rates between 20-24.9% to implement default management plans, provide additional financial aid counseling, and submit annual compliance reports. Institutions exceeding statutory thresholds face immediate loss of eligibility for new Title IV disbursements, affecting federal aid availability for current and prospective students.

The regulation encompasses default prevention strategies mandated by 20 U.S.C. § 1092(l), requiring entrance and exit counseling for federal loan recipients, debt management education, and career placement assistance. Institutions must maintain comprehensive records of borrower communications and default prevention activities for federal audit purposes under 34 CFR § 668.24.

Recent amendments through the FUTURE Act of 2019 modified data matching requirements and enhanced institutional reporting obligations for cohort default rate calculations. The regulation applies to all Title IV-eligible institutions, including public, private nonprofit, and proprietary institutions participating in federal student aid programs, with enhanced scrutiny for career training programs under 34 CFR § 668.8(d)(2).', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.549121', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-067', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

1. Maintain qualified financial aid staff and make available fifteen categories of required information through publications, mailings, and electronic media
2. Calculate and annually disclose completion and graduation rates for certificate or degree-seeking full-time undergraduate students by July 1st
3. Provide comprehensive exit counseling for all Direct Loan borrowers before graduation, withdrawal, or enrollment below half-time status
4. Publish annual campus security reports and ensure compliance with net price calculator requirements
5. Maintain current information on financial aid programs, refund policies, academic standards, and disability services', 'Student Services', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.57682', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Student Right to Know Act, codified at 20 U.S.C. § 1092 and implemented through 34 CFR § 668.41-668.49, mandates comprehensive disclosure requirements for postsecondary institutions participating in federal student aid programs under Title IV of the Higher Education Act. Enacted as Public Law No. 101-542, this legislation ensures prospective and enrolled students receive essential information to make informed educational decisions.

Under 20 U.S.C. § 1092(a), institutions must maintain qualified financial aid staff and make readily available through publications, mailings, and electronic media fifteen categories of information: (1) available financial aid programs; (2) distribution methods among recipients; (3) application requirements and procedures; (4) student rights and responsibilities; (5) cost of attendance calculations; (6) refund policies and Return to Title IV requirements; (7) academic program descriptions; (8) financial aid personnel contact information; (9) disability services and facilities; (10) accrediting agency names; (11) academic standards for aid eligibility; (12) completion and graduation rates; (13) loan deferment and cancellation provisions; (14) study abroad program aid applicability; and (15) annual campus security reports per the Clery Act.

The completion rate disclosure requirement under 20 U.S.C. § 1092(a)(1)(L) mandates annual calculation of graduation rates for certificate or degree-seeking, full-time undergraduate students entering during the most recent fall term. For NCAA member institutions, 34 CFR § 668.41(d) permits satisfaction through athletic program disclosures to prospective student-athletes, coaches, parents, and guidance counselors. Publication must occur by July 1st annually, with Spring IPEDS reporting fulfilling this deadline per 34 CFR § 668.45.

Enforcement mechanisms under 20 U.S.C. § 1094 include program review findings, compliance audits, and potential sanctions ranging from monetary fines to Title IV program suspension or termination. The Department of Education''s Federal Student Aid office conducts oversight through systematic reviews and complaint investigations. Recent amendments through the FAFSA Simplification Act have enhanced disclosure requirements for net price calculators and outcome measures, effective for the 2024-2025 award year. Exit counseling requirements under 34 CFR § 685.304 mandate comprehensive loan repayment information sessions for all Direct Loan borrowers before graduation, withdrawal, or enrollment below half-time status.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.57682', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-068', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

 	For awards made prior to 12/26/2014, EDGAR parts 74 and 80 (related to administration of grants and agreements with institutions of higher education and with state and local governments) still apply (to view prior versions of the CFR and for additional explanation, visit here). For awards made on or after 12/26/2014, 2 CFR Part 200, which includes the substance formerly in parts 74 and 80, applies.', '', 'Research', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.84613', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Department of Education General Administrative Regulations (EDGAR) establish comprehensive administrative and fiscal requirements for federal education grants administered by the U.S. Department of Education. Codified primarily in 34 CFR Parts 74-86 and supplemented by 2 CFR Part 200, these regulations govern billions in federal education funding distributed to higher education institutions annually.

**Legal Authority and Scope**
EDGAR derives authority from the General Education Provisions Act (20 U.S.C. § 1221 et seq.) and the Education Department Organization Act (20 U.S.C. § 3474). The regulations apply to direct institutional grants, state-administered programs, drug and alcohol abuse prevention initiatives under 34 CFR Part 86, and research involving human subjects under 34 CFR Part 97.

**Key Transitional Provisions**
A critical compliance distinction exists based on award dates. Awards made prior to December 26, 2014, remain governed by 34 CFR Parts 74 and 80, which established separate administrative frameworks for institutions of higher education and state/local governments respectively. Awards made on or after December 26, 2014, fall under the Uniform Administrative Requirements codified in 2 CFR Part 200, which consolidated and modernized previous requirements.

**Core Requirements for Higher Education**
Institutions must maintain comprehensive financial management systems meeting standards in 2 CFR § 200.302, including adequate internal controls, source documentation for transactions, and effective control over federal awards. Cost principles under 2 CFR Part 200, Subpart E, require institutions to ensure costs are allowable, allocable, reasonable, and consistently treated. The regulations mandate specific procurement procedures (2 CFR §§ 200.317-200.326), including competitive bidding requirements and conflict of interest prohibitions.

**Audit and Compliance Framework**
Institutions expending $750,000 or more in federal awards annually must undergo single audits under 2 CFR Part 200, Subpart F. These audits examine financial statements, compliance with federal requirements, and internal control effectiveness. Institutions must submit audit reports within nine months of fiscal year-end through the Federal Audit Clearinghouse.

**Enforcement and Remedies**
Non-compliance may result in specific conditions on awards, temporary withholding of payments, disallowance of costs, wholly or partially suspending awards, or award termination (2 CFR § 200.338). The Department may also suspend or debar institutions from future federal funding participation under 2 CFR Parts 180 and 3485.

**Recent Updates**
The 2020 amendments to 2 CFR Part 200 revised indirect cost rate negotiations, simplified certain administrative requirements, and enhanced flexibility for emergency situations, including provisions addressing COVID-19 pandemic impacts on institutional operations and student services.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.84613', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-104', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

PROHIBITIONS (31 USC 3729):
- Do not knowingly submit false or fraudulent claims for payment
- Do not make false records or statements material to claims
- Do not conspire to defraud the government
- Do not conceal obligations to pay money to the government

AFFIRMATIVE REQUIREMENT (31 USC 3730(h)):
- Protect employees, contractors, and agents from retaliation for reporting FCA violations
- Prohibited retaliation includes: discharge, demotion, suspension, threats, harassment, discrimination in employment terms
- Remedy for retaliation: reinstatement, 2x back pay, interest, special damages, attorneys fees
- Statute of limitations: 3 years from date of retaliation

NOTE: The FCA is primarily an enforcement/prohibition statute. It does not impose extensive affirmative compliance requirements like HIPAA or FERPA. Compliance means: (1) not submitting false claims, and (2) protecting whistleblowers.', 'Finance', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.874935', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The False Claims Act (FCA), codified at 31 U.S.C. §§ 3729-3733, represents one of the federal government''s most powerful tools for combating fraud against federal programs, with significant implications for higher education institutions receiving federal funding. Originally enacted during the Civil War and substantially amended by the False Claims Amendments Act of 1986 and the Fraud Enforcement and Recovery Act of 2009, the FCA imposes civil liability on any person who knowingly submits, or causes the submission of, false or fraudulent claims for payment to the United States government.

Under 31 U.S.C. § 3729(a)(1), liability attaches when an individual or entity "knowingly presents, or causes to be presented, a false or fraudulent claim for payment or approval." The statute defines "knowingly" broadly under 31 U.S.C. § 3729(b)(1) to include actual knowledge, deliberate ignorance, or reckless disregard for the truth or falsity of information, establishing a scienter standard that does not require specific intent to defraud.

For higher education institutions, FCA exposure is particularly acute given their extensive receipt of federal funding through Title IV student financial aid programs (20 U.S.C. § 1070 et seq.), federal research grants from agencies like NSF and NIH, and other federal contracts. Common FCA violations in higher education include: inflated indirect cost claims under federal grants, false certification of compliance with federal regulations for continued program eligibility, misrepresentation of student enrollment status affecting financial aid disbursements, and submission of inaccurate effort reporting for federally-funded research personnel.

The FCA''s enforcement mechanisms are particularly robust. Under 31 U.S.C. § 3729(a)(1), violators face civil penalties of $13,508 to $27,018 per false claim (as adjusted for inflation under 31 U.S.C. § 3729(a)(1) and 28 CFR § 85.3), plus treble damages representing three times the government''s actual damages. The statute''s unique qui tam provision under 31 U.S.C. § 3730 allows private whistleblowers (relators) to file lawsuits on behalf of the government, with successful relators entitled to 15-30% of any recovery.

Recent amendments have expanded FCA liability significantly. The 2009 FERA eliminated the requirement that false statements be made directly to the government, encompassing false statements made to third-party contractors or grantees. The 2010 Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act clarified that retention of overpayments constitutes a false claim under 31 U.S.C. § 3729(a)(1)(G).

Higher education institutions must implement comprehensive compliance programs addressing grant management, financial aid administration, research integrity, and regulatory compliance to mitigate FCA exposure and ensure adherence to all federal program requirements.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.874935', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-011', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

Under 2 CFR § 200.205, higher education institutions must understand that FAPIIS contains both government-entered information and contractor/grantee self-reported data. Government entries include administrative agreements, suspension and debarment actions, non-responsibility determinations, terminations for cause or default, and other performance-related findings from the past five years. The Federal Acquisition Regulation (FAR) at 48 CFR § 9.104-6 requires contracting officers to review FAPIIS records when making responsibility determinations.

For universities, FAPIIS implications extend beyond initial award considerations. The system impacts institutional reputation and future funding opportunities, as negative entries can influence agency decisions on subsequent awards. Institutions must monitor their FAPIIS records through the System for Award Management (SAM) portal, where they can access their performance information and submit comments or corrections regarding government entries within 30 days of notification per 41 U.S.C. § 2313(c)(2).

Compliance obligations include maintaining accurate registration in SAM, promptly addressing any adverse findings that could result in FAPIIS entries, and implementing robust grants management systems to prevent performance issues. The General Services Administration (GSA) maintains FAPIIS as part of SAM, with data retention periods of five years for most entries. Universities should establish internal procedures for monitoring their FAPIIS status, particularly when pursuing large federal awards or renewals.

Enforcement mechanisms include potential award denials, increased oversight conditions, or suspension and debarment proceedings for institutions with significant integrity concerns. Recent updates under the Federal Acquisition Supply Chain Security Act have expanded reporting requirements for certain technology-related contracts, affecting universities with federal research contracts involving covered telecommunications equipment or services.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.903543', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-111', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

 	For each award the University must include: 1) Name of receiving entity; 2) amount; 3) transaction type, funding agency and program source; 4) location of recipient entity and primary location of performance; 5) reporting on executive compensation for first-tier sub award recipients; and 6) unique recipient entity identifier.

 	Reporting requirements apply to vendors as well as sub-grantees.', '', 'Research', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.931438', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Federal Funding Accountability and Transparency Act of 2006 (FFATA), codified at 31 U.S.C. § 6101 et seq., represents a landmark transparency initiative requiring comprehensive disclosure of federal spending through a centralized, publicly accessible database. For higher education institutions, this statute imposes significant reporting obligations that extend beyond the institution itself to include sub-recipients and vendors.

Enacted as Public Law 109-282 and subsequently amended by the Government Funding Transparency Act (P.L. 110-252, § 6202), FFATA mandates that all federal awards exceeding $25,000 must be reported to USASpending.gov within 30 days of obligation. Universities receiving federal grants, contracts, or cooperative agreements must ensure complete transparency regarding fund distribution and utilization.

Key provisions under 31 U.S.C. § 6102 require institutions to report: (1) the name and unique identifier of each receiving entity; (2) award amounts and funding sources; (3) transaction types and responsible federal agencies; (4) recipient locations and primary performance sites; (5) executive compensation data for sub-recipients receiving $25,000+ annually; and (6) North American Industry Classification System codes where applicable.

The Act''s scope encompasses all federal funding streams common to higher education, including research grants from NSF, NIH, and DOE, as well as student aid administrative funds and facilities grants. Compliance obligations extend to sub-awards, creating a cascading responsibility where universities must ensure sub-recipients also meet FFATA requirements.

Enforcement mechanisms include award suspension, termination, and debarment procedures outlined in 2 CFR Part 180. The Federal Funding Accountability and Transparency Act Subaward Reporting System (FSRS) serves as the primary reporting mechanism for sub-award data.

Recent amendments through the DATA Act (P.L. 113-101) enhanced reporting requirements and established standardized data elements, requiring universities to maintain more granular financial tracking systems to ensure compliance with evolving transparency mandates.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.931438', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-112', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

 	FERA also expands the False Claims Act provisions to sub-recipients of federal funds.', '', 'Research', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.96059', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Fraud Enforcement and Recovery Act of 2009 (FERA), codified at 31 U.S.C. § 3729-3733, significantly strengthened the False Claims Act (FCA) by expanding liability for fraudulent claims involving federal funds. For higher education institutions, FERA creates comprehensive obligations regarding the accurate use and reporting of federal grants, contracts, and financial aid programs.

FERA clarifies that liability under 31 U.S.C. § 3729(a)(1)(A) extends to any false or fraudulent claim for government money or property, regardless of whether the claim is presented directly to a government official, whether the government has physical custody of funds, or whether the defendant specifically intended to defraud the government. This expansion is particularly significant for universities receiving federal research grants, Title IV financial aid, and other federal funding streams.

Key provisions under 31 U.S.C. § 3729(a)(1)(B) expand liability to sub-recipients of federal funds, meaning that universities must ensure compliance not only in their direct federal dealings but also in their oversight of subcontractors, research partners, and third-party service providers. Universities receiving federal funds through programs administered under 20 U.S.C. Chapter 28 (Higher Education Act) and 42 U.S.C. Chapter 16 (National Science Foundation) face enhanced scrutiny.

FERA reduces the knowledge standard from "specific intent" to "knowing" conduct, defined under 31 U.S.C. § 3729(b)(1)(A) as actual knowledge, deliberate ignorance, or reckless disregard of truth or falsity. Universities must implement robust internal controls to prevent inadvertent violations in grant reporting, student enrollment certifications, and research expenditure documentation.

Enforcement mechanisms include civil penalties of $11,803 to $23,607 per false claim (adjusted annually under 28 CFR § 85.5), plus treble damages. The Department of Justice and various agency Inspectors General actively pursue FERA violations in higher education, particularly involving research misconduct, improper cost transfers, and financial aid fraud.

Whistleblower protections under 31 U.S.C. § 3730(h) encourage reporting of violations, with qui tam relators eligible for 15-30% of recovered funds. Universities must establish anti-retaliation policies and internal reporting mechanisms to address potential violations before they escalate to federal enforcement actions.

Recent enforcement trends focus on cybersecurity grant compliance, COVID-19 relief fund usage, and research integrity violations, requiring universities to maintain comprehensive documentation and implement regular compliance auditing procedures.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.96059', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-113', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

 	All substances are placed in one of five schedules, based on medicinal value, harmfulness, and potential for abuse or addiction, with Schedule 1 reserved for the most dangerous drugs that have no recognized medical use.', '', 'Human Resources', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.989901', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Controlled Substances Act (CSA) of 1970, codified at 21 U.S.C. §§ 801-889, establishes a comprehensive federal regulatory framework for controlled substances that significantly impacts higher education institutions. The Act creates five schedules of controlled substances based on medical utility, potential for abuse, and safety considerations, with Schedule I representing the most restrictive category for substances with no accepted medical use and high abuse potential.

Higher education institutions must comply with multiple CSA provisions across research, healthcare, and campus safety contexts. Under 21 U.S.C. § 822, universities conducting research with controlled substances must obtain DEA registration for each principal investigator and research location. The registration process requires detailed security protocols, storage requirements, and personnel background checks as specified in 21 CFR Parts 1301-1321.

Institutions operating health centers, veterinary programs, or medical schools face additional obligations under 21 U.S.C. § 823. Licensed practitioners must maintain separate registrations, implement proper prescription monitoring systems, and establish comprehensive record-keeping procedures. The Act requires detailed inventory controls under 21 CFR § 1304.11, mandating biennial physical inventories and perpetual inventory systems for Schedule I-II substances.

Campus security considerations arise under 21 U.S.C. § 881, which permits asset forfeiture for property used in drug-related activities. Universities must implement robust security measures including 21 CFR § 1301.72 requirements for physical safeguards, surveillance systems, and access controls. Storage facilities must meet specific construction standards with time-delay safes for Schedule I-II substances and steel cabinets for lower schedules.

Violation penalties under 21 U.S.C. § 842 include substantial civil and criminal sanctions. Administrative penalties range from $10,000 to $100,000 per violation, while criminal penalties may include imprisonment up to 20 years depending on the substance schedule and quantity involved. The DEA may suspend or revoke institutional registrations under 21 U.S.C. § 824 for non-compliance.

Recent amendments through the SUPPORT Act (P.L. 115-271) expanded reporting requirements for suspicious orders and enhanced prescription monitoring obligations. The Comprehensive Addiction and Recovery Act modifications under 21 U.S.C. § 822a require additional training certifications for prescribing practitioners. Universities must ensure compliance with state-level prescription drug monitoring programs as mandated by 21 U.S.C. § 831.

Enforcement mechanisms include routine DEA inspections, unannounced audits, and compliance investigations. The Agency maintains authority under 21 U.S.C. § 878 to conduct administrative inspections of registered facilities, examine records, and interview personnel. Non-compliance triggers formal enforcement proceedings potentially resulting in registration revocation, civil monetary penalties, and criminal referrals to federal prosecutors.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:13.989901', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-102', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

 	A license application must be filed with the FCC.', '', 'Information Technology', 'federal', true, NULL, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:14.343027', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', '', '', NULL, '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'The Federal Communications Commission (FCC) regulates wireless communications licensing for higher education institutions under 47 U.S.C. § 151 et seq. and implementing regulations in 47 CFR Parts 1, 20, 22, 24, 27, and 90. Universities seeking to provide wireless communication services must obtain appropriate FCC authorization through a comprehensive licensing framework that governs spectrum use, equipment deployment, and service provision.

Under 47 U.S.C. § 301, no person shall use or operate any apparatus for the transmission of energy or communications by radio without a license issued by the FCC. Higher education institutions may apply for licenses covering four distinct service categories: (1) common carrier services under 47 CFR Part 22 for commercial telecommunications offerings; (2) non-common carrier services under 47 CFR Part 101 for point-to-point microwave communications; (3) private internal communications under 47 CFR Part 90 for campus security, facilities management, and administrative operations; and (4) broadcast services under 47 CFR Part 73 for educational radio and television stations.

The licensing process requires submission of FCC Form 601 through the Universal Licensing System (ULS), with specific technical showings demonstrating frequency coordination, interference protection, and compliance with RF exposure limits under 47 CFR § 1.1307. Universities must provide detailed engineering studies showing antenna patterns, effective radiated power, and coverage areas. For educational broadcast services, institutions must file FCC Form 2100 demonstrating compliance with public interest obligations under 47 U.S.C. § 309(a).

Key compliance requirements include maintaining accurate station records per 47 CFR § 1.913, conducting annual equipment inspections, and ensuring operators hold appropriate licenses under 47 CFR Part 97. Universities must also comply with environmental assessment requirements under 47 CFR § 1.1301 for antenna installations and demonstrate compliance with local zoning ordinances.

Enforcement mechanisms include monetary forfeitures up to $100,000 per violation under 47 U.S.C. § 503(b), license revocation proceedings, and criminal penalties for willful violations. The FCC conducts regular compliance audits and responds to interference complaints. Recent amendments through the Spectrum Frontiers proceeding have expanded millimeter wave opportunities for campus wireless networks under 47 CFR Part 30.

Universities must renew licenses every ten years for most services, with renewal applications due between 90-120 days before expiration. License modifications require prior FCC approval for changes in frequency, power, or antenna specifications under 47 CFR § 1.929.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, NULL, '2026-06-25 15:35:14.343027', NULL, true, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '', NULL, NULL, 'federal', NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'medium', false, 'annual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'REG-110', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL);

1. PRIVACY RULE (45 CFR Part 164 Subpart E)
   - Privacy Officer designation
   - Notice of Privacy Practices
   - Authorization for non-permitted uses/disclosures
   - Minimum necessary standard
   - Patient rights (access, amendment, accounting)
   - De-identification procedures

2. SECURITY RULE (45 CFR Part 164 Subpart C)
   - Security Officer designation
   - Risk analysis and management
   - Administrative safeguards
   - Physical safeguards
   - Technical safeguards (access control, audit, encryption)
   - Workstation and device security

3. BREACH NOTIFICATION RULE (45 CFR Part 164 Subpart D)
